window.LESSONS = [{"id":"js-1","week":1,"session":1,"lang":"js","title":"Hello, JavaScript","minutes":60,"summary":"Run your first code, store values in variables, and do maths and text with them.","goals":["Run code and print results with <code>console.log</code>","Tell numbers, strings and booleans apart","Store values with <code>let</code> and <code>const</code>","Build text with template literals"],"sections":[{"type":"step","title":"What JavaScript is","html":"<p>JavaScript is the programming language of the web. Every website you use runs it, and it also runs servers, apps and tools.</p>\n<p>TypeScript, the goal of this course, is JavaScript <strong>plus types</strong>. So we learn JavaScript first. Everything you learn in weeks 1 and 2 still works in TypeScript.</p>\n<p>Press <strong>Run</strong> on the code below. Then change the text between the quotes and run it again.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>console.log(&quot;Hello, world!&quot;);</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p><code>console.log(...)</code> prints whatever you put between the brackets. It is the main way you will see what your code is doing. Programmers use it all the time, not just beginners.</p>\n<p>Each line of code is a <strong>statement</strong>, an instruction for the computer. Statements run from top to bottom, one after another. The semicolon <code>;</code> at the end marks where a statement finishes. JavaScript often lets you leave it out, but we will always write it, which avoids a few rare surprises.</p>\n</div>\n<aside class=\"tip\"><p>You can also press <strong>Ctrl + Enter</strong> (or <strong>⌘ + Enter</strong> on a Mac) inside any editor to run it.</p>\n</aside>"},{"type":"step","title":"Printing and comments","html":"<p><code>console.log</code> can print several things at once if you separate them with commas. Lines that start with <code>//</code> are <strong>comments</strong>. JavaScript ignores them; they are notes for humans.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>// This line is a comment. It does nothing.\nconsole.log(&quot;I am&quot;, 30, &quot;years old&quot;);\nconsole.log(2 + 3);\n\n/* A comment can also\n   span several lines like this. */\nconsole.log(&quot;Done&quot;);</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>Notice that <code>2 + 3</code> printed <code>5</code>. JavaScript worked out the sum before printing. Anything that produces a value (like <code>2 + 3</code>) is called an <strong>expression</strong>.</p>\n<p>Use comments to explain <em>why</em> code does something. Don&#39;t use them to repeat what the code obviously says.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"Values and their types","html":"<p>Every value in JavaScript has a <strong>type</strong>. The three you will use most:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Examples</th>\n<th>Used for</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>number</code></td>\n<td><code>42</code>, <code>3.14</code>, <code>-7</code></td>\n<td>counting, maths, money, measurements</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>string</code></td>\n<td><code>&quot;hello&quot;</code>, <code>&#39;Ada&#39;</code></td>\n<td>text of any kind</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>boolean</code></td>\n<td><code>true</code>, <code>false</code></td>\n<td>yes/no answers</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><code>typeof</code> tells you the type of a value. Try it:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>console.log(typeof 42);\nconsole.log(typeof &quot;42&quot;);\nconsole.log(typeof true);\nconsole.log(typeof 3.5);</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>Strings can use double quotes <code>&quot;...&quot;</code> or single quotes <code>&#39;...&#39;</code>. Pick one and stay consistent. There is a third kind using backticks, which you will meet soon.</p>\n<p><code>42</code> and <code>&quot;42&quot;</code> look alike but are different types. One is a number you can do maths with, the other is text that happens to contain digits. This difference causes many bugs, and catching bugs like it is a big part of why TypeScript exists.</p>\n<p>JavaScript has no separate type for whole numbers and decimals. Both are just <code>number</code>.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"quiz","title":"Quick check","question":"<p>What does <code>typeof &quot;42&quot;</code> give you?</p>\n","options":["<code>&quot;number&quot;</code>","<code>&quot;string&quot;</code>","<code>&quot;boolean&quot;</code>","<code>&quot;42&quot;</code>"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>The quotes make it a string, even though it contains digits.</p>\n"},{"type":"step","title":"Variables with let and const","html":"<p>A <strong>variable</strong> is a name that holds a value, so you can use it later. Create one with <code>const</code> or <code>let</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>const name = &quot;Ada&quot;;\nlet score = 10;\n\nconsole.log(name, &quot;has&quot;, score, &quot;points&quot;);\n\nscore = score + 5;   // let can be changed later\nconsole.log(name, &quot;now has&quot;, score, &quot;points&quot;);</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><ul>\n<li><code>const</code> means the variable can <strong>not</strong> be reassigned. Use it by default.</li>\n<li><code>let</code> means you can give the variable a new value later. Use it only when you actually need to change the value.</li>\n<li>You may see <code>var</code> in older code. It is the old way of making variables and has confusing rules. Don&#39;t use it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <code>=</code> sign does not mean &quot;equals&quot; like in maths. It means &quot;store the value on the right in the name on the left&quot;. So <code>score = score + 5</code> means &quot;take the current score, add 5, and store the result back in score&quot;.</p>\n</div>\n<p>Now see what happens when you try to change a <code>const</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>const birthYear = 1990;\nbirthYear = 1991;\nconsole.log(birthYear);</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>You get a <code>TypeError: Assignment to constant variable.</code> Errors are normal and useful. Read the message: it usually tells you exactly what went wrong. The program stops at the line with the error, so the <code>console.log</code> never runs.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"Naming things","html":"<p>Variable names can contain letters, digits, <code>_</code> and <code>$</code>, but can&#39;t start with a digit. JavaScript programmers write multi-word names in <strong>camelCase</strong>: first word lowercase, each next word capitalised.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>const firstName = &quot;Grace&quot;;\nconst itemsInCart = 3;\nconst isLoggedIn = true;\nconsole.log(firstName, itemsInCart, isLoggedIn);</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>Good names describe what is inside: <code>itemsInCart</code> is better than <code>n</code> or <code>x</code>. Booleans often start with <code>is</code>, <code>has</code> or <code>can</code>, like <code>isLoggedIn</code> or <code>hasDiscount</code>, so they read like questions.</p>\n<p>Names are case-sensitive: <code>score</code> and <code>Score</code> are two different variables. Some words, like <code>let</code>, <code>const</code>, <code>if</code> and <code>function</code>, are reserved by the language and can&#39;t be used as names.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"Doing maths","html":"<p>JavaScript works like a calculator. The usual order of operations applies, and brackets go first.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>console.log(10 + 3);   // add\nconsole.log(10 - 3);   // subtract\nconsole.log(10 * 3);   // multiply\nconsole.log(10 / 3);   // divide\nconsole.log(10 % 3);   // remainder after dividing\nconsole.log(2 ** 3);   // power: 2 × 2 × 2\nconsole.log(2 + 3 * 4, (2 + 3) * 4);</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>The remainder operator <code>%</code> is more useful than it looks. <code>n % 2</code> is <code>0</code> for even numbers and <code>1</code> for odd ones, which you will use in the next session.</p>\n<p>There are short ways to update a variable:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>let count = 10;\ncount += 5;    // same as count = count + 5\ncount -= 2;    // same as count = count - 2\ncount *= 2;    // same as count = count * 2\ncount++;       // add 1\nconsole.log(count);</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>Math</code> holds useful helpers: <code>Math.round(2.6)</code> is <code>3</code>, <code>Math.floor(2.6)</code> rounds down to <code>2</code>, <code>Math.max(4, 9, 2)</code> is <code>9</code>, and <code>Math.random()</code> gives a random number between 0 and 1.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"Working with text","html":"<p>You can join strings with <code>+</code>, but <strong>template literals</strong> are easier. Use backticks <code>`</code> and put any expression inside <code>${ }</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>const city = &quot;Lisbon&quot;;\nconst days = 4;\n\nconsole.log(&quot;I am going to &quot; + city + &quot; for &quot; + days + &quot; days.&quot;);\nconsole.log(`I am going to ${city} for ${days} days.`);\nconsole.log(`That is ${days * 24} hours!`);</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>Strings also come with built-in tools, called <strong>methods</strong>, that you call with a dot:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>const word = &quot;JavaScript&quot;;\nconsole.log(word.length);           // how many characters\nconsole.log(word.toUpperCase());\nconsole.log(word.toLowerCase());\nconsole.log(word.includes(&quot;Script&quot;));\nconsole.log(word[0]);                // first character (counting starts at 0)\nconsole.log(word.slice(0, 4));       // characters 0 to 3</code></pre></div>\n<p>Counting from <code>0</code> is the norm in programming. The first character is at position <code>0</code>, the second at <code>1</code>, and so on.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"When types mix","html":"<p>JavaScript tries to be helpful when you mix types, and the result is sometimes surprising. With a string involved, <code>+</code> joins text instead of adding numbers.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>console.log(&quot;5&quot; + 3);\nconsole.log(&quot;5&quot; - 3);\nconsole.log(Number(&quot;5&quot;) + 3);\nconsole.log(Number(&quot;hello&quot;));\nconsole.log(String(42) + &quot;!&quot;);</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><ul>\n<li><code>&quot;5&quot; + 3</code> gives <code>&quot;53&quot;</code> because <code>+</code> with a string means &quot;join&quot;.</li>\n<li><code>&quot;5&quot; - 3</code> gives <code>2</code> because <code>-</code> only works on numbers, so JavaScript converts the string.</li>\n<li><code>Number(...)</code> converts something to a number. If that fails you get <code>NaN</code>, short for &quot;Not a Number&quot;.</li>\n<li><code>String(...)</code> converts something to a string.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Silent conversions like these are a classic source of bugs. In week 3, TypeScript will warn you about many of them before your code even runs.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"quiz","title":"Quick check","question":"<p>What does <code>console.log(&quot;2&quot; + 2)</code> print?</p>\n","options":["<code>4</code>","<code>22</code>","<code>&quot;4&quot;</code>","An error"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>When one side of <code>+</code> is a string, JavaScript joins them as text: <code>&quot;2&quot;</code> and <code>&quot;2&quot;</code> become <code>&quot;22&quot;</code>.</p>\n"},{"type":"step","title":"Nothing: undefined and null","html":"<p>Two special values mean &quot;nothing here&quot;:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>undefined</code> means a value was never set.</li>\n<li><code>null</code> means &quot;empty on purpose&quot;, set by a programmer.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>let nickname;\nconsole.log(nickname);\n\nlet middleName = null;\nconsole.log(middleName);\n\nconsole.log(typeof undefined, typeof null);</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p><code>typeof null</code> printing <code>&quot;object&quot;</code> is a famous mistake from the first version of JavaScript that can never be fixed without breaking old websites. Just remember that <code>null</code> is its own thing.</p>\n<p>You now know JavaScript&#39;s basic types: <code>number</code>, <code>string</code>, <code>boolean</code>, <code>undefined</code> and <code>null</code>. Objects and arrays come in week 2.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"quiz","title":"Quick check","question":"<p>Which line causes an error?</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"static\"><pre><code>let a = 1;\nconst b = 2;\na = 3;\nb = 4;</code></pre></div>\n","options":["<code>let a = 1;</code>","<code>const b = 2;</code>","<code>a = 3;</code>","<code>b = 4;</code>"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p><code>b</code> was declared with <code>const</code>, so it can&#39;t be reassigned. Changing <code>a</code> is fine because it was declared with <code>let</code>.</p>\n"},{"type":"exercise","id":"js-1-ex1","title":"Introduce yourself","lang":"js","starter":"// Create your variables here\n\n\n// Print the sentence here\n","tests":"test(\"myName is a string\", () => expect(myName).toBeType(\"string\"));\ntest(\"age is a number\", () => expect(age).toBeType(\"number\"));\ntest(\"the sentence is printed exactly\", () =>\n  expect($logs).toContain(`My name is ${myName} and I am ${age} years old.`));","solution":"const myName = \"Sam\";\nlet age = 31;\n\nconsole.log(`My name is ${myName} and I am ${age} years old.`);","hints":["<p>A template literal uses backticks: <code>`My name is ${myName} ...`</code></p>\n","<p>Watch the details: the full stop at the end, and the spaces around the values.</p>\n"],"html":"<p>Create two variables:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a <code>const</code> called <code>myName</code> holding your name (a string)</li>\n<li>a <code>let</code> called <code>age</code> holding your age (a number)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Then print exactly this sentence using a template literal, with your own values:</p>\n<p><code>My name is Sam and I am 31 years old.</code></p>\n"},{"type":"exercise","id":"js-1-ex2","title":"Fix the bugs","lang":"js","starter":"const score = 10;\nscore = score + 5;\n\nconst bonus = \"5\";\nconst total = score + bonus;\n\nconsole.log(total);","tests":"test(\"score ends up as 15\", () => expect(score).toBe(15));\ntest(\"total is the number 20\", () => expect(total).toBe(20));","solution":"let score = 10;\nscore = score + 5;\n\nconst bonus = 5;\nconst total = score + bonus;\n\nconsole.log(total);","hints":["<p>The first error is about <code>const</code>. Which keyword lets a variable change?</p>\n","<p>Once that is fixed, the output is <code>155</code> instead of <code>20</code>. Look at the type of <code>bonus</code>.</p>\n"],"html":"<p>This code should print <code>20</code>, but it has two bugs. Run it, read the error, and fix it. Keep the variable names the same.</p>\n"},{"type":"exercise","id":"js-1-ex3","title":"Temperature converter","lang":"js","starter":"const celsius = 25;\n\n// Your code here\n","tests":"test(\"fahrenheit is 77\", () => expect(fahrenheit).toBe(77));\ntest(\"prints '25°C is 77°F'\", () => expect($logs).toContain(\"25°C is 77°F\"));","solution":"const celsius = 25;\n\nconst fahrenheit = celsius * 9 / 5 + 32;\nconsole.log(`${celsius}°C is ${fahrenheit}°F`);","hints":["<p><code>const fahrenheit = celsius * 9 / 5 + 32;</code></p>\n"],"html":"<p>A <code>celsius</code> variable is given. Create a <code>const</code> called <code>fahrenheit</code> using the formula</p>\n<p><strong>F = C × 9 / 5 + 32</strong></p>\n<p>Then print a line like <code>25°C is 77°F</code>.</p>\n"},{"type":"exercise","id":"js-1-ex4","title":"Hours, minutes, seconds","lang":"js","starter":"const totalSeconds = 3725;\n\n// Your code here\n\nconsole.log(`${hours}h ${minutes}m ${seconds}s`);","tests":"test(\"hours is 1\", () => expect(hours).toBe(1));\ntest(\"minutes is 2\", () => expect(minutes).toBe(2));\ntest(\"seconds is 5\", () => expect(seconds).toBe(5));","solution":"const totalSeconds = 3725;\n\nconst hours = Math.floor(totalSeconds / 3600);\nconst minutes = Math.floor((totalSeconds % 3600) / 60);\nconst seconds = totalSeconds % 60;\n\nconsole.log(`${hours}h ${minutes}m ${seconds}s`);","hints":["<p>There are 3600 seconds in an hour. <code>Math.floor</code> rounds down to a whole number.</p>\n","<p><code>%</code> gives the remainder. <code>totalSeconds % 3600</code> is the seconds left over after taking out the whole hours.</p>\n"],"html":"<p><code>totalSeconds</code> holds a duration. Work out how many whole <code>hours</code>, <code>minutes</code> and <code>seconds</code> that is, and store each in a <code>const</code> with those names.</p>\n<p>For 3725 seconds the answer is 1 hour, 2 minutes and 5 seconds.</p>\n"}]},{"id":"js-2","week":1,"session":2,"lang":"js","title":"Decisions and loops","minutes":60,"summary":"Make your code choose between paths with <code>if</code>, and repeat work with loops.","goals":["Compare values with <code>===</code>, <code>&lt;</code> and friends","Choose what runs with <code>if</code>, <code>else if</code> and <code>else</code>","Combine conditions with <code>&amp;&amp;</code>, `","","<code>and</code>!`","Repeat code with <code>for</code>, <code>while</code> and <code>for...of</code>"],"sections":[{"type":"step","title":"Comparing values","html":"<p>A <strong>comparison</strong> asks a yes/no question and gives back a boolean: <code>true</code> or <code>false</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>const age = 20;\nconsole.log(age &gt; 18);     // greater than\nconsole.log(age &lt; 18);     // less than\nconsole.log(age &gt;= 20);    // greater than or equal\nconsole.log(age &lt;= 19);    // less than or equal\nconsole.log(age === 20);   // equal\nconsole.log(age !== 20);   // not equal</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>Remember: a single <code>=</code> stores a value. To <strong>compare</strong>, use three: <code>===</code>. To check &quot;not equal&quot;, use <code>!==</code>.</p>\n<p>Strings can be compared too. <code>&quot;apple&quot; === &quot;apple&quot;</code> is <code>true</code>, and comparisons are case-sensitive, so <code>&quot;Apple&quot; === &quot;apple&quot;</code> is <code>false</code>.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"Why === and not ==","html":"<p>JavaScript also has <code>==</code> (two equals signs). It converts types before comparing, which leads to strange results. Always use <code>===</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>console.log(5 == &quot;5&quot;);    // true  (converted!)\nconsole.log(5 === &quot;5&quot;);   // false (number vs string)\nconsole.log(0 == &quot;&quot;);     // true  (surprise)\nconsole.log(0 === &quot;&quot;);    // false</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p><code>===</code> is called <strong>strict equality</strong>: both the type and the value must match. Linters (tools that check code style) and TypeScript projects almost always require <code>===</code>, so get into the habit now.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"Making decisions with if","html":"<p><code>if</code> runs a block of code only when a condition is <code>true</code>. <code>else</code> runs when it isn&#39;t.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>const temperature = 28;\n\nif (temperature &gt; 25) {\n  console.log(&quot;It's hot. Wear shorts.&quot;);\n} else {\n  console.log(&quot;Bring a jacket.&quot;);\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>Change <code>temperature</code> to <code>12</code> and run it again.</p>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>The condition goes in round brackets <code>( )</code>. The code to run goes in curly braces <code>{ }</code>, called a <strong>block</strong>. Code inside a block is usually indented by two spaces so it is easy to see what belongs to what.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"More than two choices","html":"<p>Chain conditions with <code>else if</code>. JavaScript checks them from top to bottom and runs <strong>only the first</strong> one that is true.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>const hour = 15;\n\nif (hour &lt; 12) {\n  console.log(&quot;Good morning&quot;);\n} else if (hour &lt; 18) {\n  console.log(&quot;Good afternoon&quot;);\n} else {\n  console.log(&quot;Good evening&quot;);\n}</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>Order matters. If you put <code>hour &lt; 18</code> first, it would also catch the morning hours, and &quot;Good morning&quot; could never be printed.</p>\n<p>When you compare one value against many fixed options, a <code>switch</code> statement can be tidier:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>const day = &quot;sat&quot;;\n\nswitch (day) {\n  case &quot;sat&quot;:\n  case &quot;sun&quot;:\n    console.log(&quot;Weekend!&quot;);\n    break;\n  case &quot;fri&quot;:\n    console.log(&quot;Almost there&quot;);\n    break;\n  default:\n    console.log(&quot;Work day&quot;);\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>Each <code>case</code> needs a <code>break</code>, otherwise the code keeps running into the next case. Most of the time <code>if</code>/<code>else if</code> is all you need.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"Combining conditions","html":"<ul>\n<li><code>&amp;&amp;</code> (and): true only if <strong>both</strong> sides are true</li>\n<li><code>||</code> (or): true if <strong>at least one</strong> side is true</li>\n<li><code>!</code> (not): flips true to false and false to true</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>const age = 25;\nconst hasTicket = true;\n\nconsole.log(age &gt;= 18 &amp;&amp; hasTicket);   // adult AND has a ticket\nconsole.log(age &lt; 12 || age &gt; 65);     // child OR senior\nconsole.log(!hasTicket);               // NOT has ticket\n\nif (age &gt;= 18 &amp;&amp; hasTicket) {\n  console.log(&quot;Come in!&quot;);\n}</code></pre></div>\n"},{"type":"step","title":"Truthy and falsy","html":"<p>An <code>if</code> doesn&#39;t need a real boolean. JavaScript treats some values as false. These are called <strong>falsy</strong>:</p>\n<p><code>false</code>, <code>0</code>, <code>&quot;&quot;</code> (empty string), <code>null</code>, <code>undefined</code> and <code>NaN</code>.</p>\n<p>Everything else is <strong>truthy</strong>, including <code>&quot;0&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;false&quot;</code> and <code>-1</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>const username = &quot;&quot;;\n\nif (username) {\n  console.log(`Welcome, ${username}`);\n} else {\n  console.log(&quot;Please enter a name&quot;);\n}\n\nconsole.log(Boolean(&quot;hello&quot;), Boolean(&quot;&quot;), Boolean(0), Boolean(&quot;0&quot;));</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>This is handy for checks like &quot;is this string empty?&quot;, but it can also bite: a count of <code>0</code> is falsy, so <code>if (count)</code> is false when the count is zero, which may not be what you meant.</p>\n<p><code>||</code> is often used to give a fallback value: <code>const shown = username || &quot;Guest&quot;;</code>. Next week you will meet <code>??</code>, which does a similar job more safely.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"The ternary operator","html":"<p>For small either/or choices that produce a value, there is a short form: <code>condition ? valueIfTrue : valueIfFalse</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>const points = 72;\nconst result = points &gt;= 50 ? &quot;pass&quot; : &quot;fail&quot;;\nconsole.log(result);\n\nconst items = 1;\nconsole.log(`You have ${items} ${items === 1 ? &quot;item&quot; : &quot;items&quot;}`);</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>Use a ternary when it stays readable on one line. If you need more than two outcomes, an <code>if</code>/<code>else if</code> chain is clearer.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"quiz","title":"Quick check","question":"<p>What does this print?</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"static\"><pre><code>const n = 0;\nif (n) {\n  console.log(&quot;A&quot;);\n} else {\n  console.log(&quot;B&quot;);\n}</code></pre></div>\n","options":["<code>A</code>","<code>B</code>","Nothing","An error"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p><code>0</code> is falsy, so the <code>else</code> branch runs and prints <code>B</code>.</p>\n"},{"type":"step","title":"Repeating with while","html":"<p>A <code>while</code> loop keeps running its block as long as its condition is true.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>let countdown = 5;\n\nwhile (countdown &gt; 0) {\n  console.log(countdown);\n  countdown--;\n}\nconsole.log(&quot;Lift off!&quot;);</code></pre></div>\n\n<aside class=\"tip warn\"><p>Make sure the condition eventually becomes false. If you forget <code>countdown--</code>, the loop never ends. That is called an <strong>infinite loop</strong>. In this site it gets stopped after 5 seconds; in a real program it would freeze.</p>\n</aside>"},{"type":"step","title":"The for loop","html":"<p>The <code>for</code> loop packs the three parts of a counting loop into one line: <strong>start</strong>; <strong>keep going while</strong>; <strong>step</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>for (let i = 1; i &lt;= 5; i++) {\n  console.log(`Lap ${i}`);\n}</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>Read it as: start with <code>i = 1</code>; keep going while <code>i &lt;= 5</code>; after each round add 1 to <code>i</code>.</p>\n<p>Programmers often call the counter <code>i</code> (for &quot;index&quot;). You can count in other steps too:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>for (let i = 10; i &gt;= 0; i -= 2) {\n  console.log(i);\n}</code></pre></div>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"Looping over a string with for...of","html":"<p><code>for...of</code> gives you each item in turn, with no counter needed. It works on strings (one character at a time) and, next week, on lists.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>const word = &quot;loop&quot;;\n\nfor (const letter of word) {\n  console.log(letter.toUpperCase());\n}</code></pre></div>\n"},{"type":"step","title":"break and continue","html":"<ul>\n<li><code>break</code> stops the loop completely.</li>\n<li><code>continue</code> skips the rest of this round and goes on to the next.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>for (let i = 1; i &lt;= 10; i++) {\n  if (i % 2 === 0) {\n    continue;          // skip even numbers\n  }\n  if (i &gt; 7) {\n    break;             // stop once we pass 7\n  }\n  console.log(i);\n}</code></pre></div>\n"},{"type":"quiz","title":"Quick check","question":"<p>How many times does this loop print?</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"static\"><pre><code>for (let i = 0; i &lt; 3; i++) {\n  console.log(&quot;hi&quot;);\n}</code></pre></div>\n","options":["2","3","4","Forever"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p><code>i</code> takes the values 0, 1 and 2. When it reaches 3, <code>i &lt; 3</code> is false and the loop stops.</p>\n"},{"type":"step","title":"A note about the exercises","html":"<p>From now on most exercises ask you to <strong>finish a function</strong>. You will learn exactly how functions work in the next session. For now:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Write your code between the curly braces <code>{ }</code>.</li>\n<li>The values you are given (like <code>score</code>) are ready to use inside.</li>\n<li>Use <code>return</code> to hand back the answer: <code>return &quot;A&quot;;</code></li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>function describeNumber(n) {\n  if (n &gt; 0) {\n    return &quot;positive&quot;;\n  }\n  return &quot;zero or negative&quot;;\n}\n\nconsole.log(describeNumber(5));\nconsole.log(describeNumber(-3));</code></pre></div>\n"},{"type":"exercise","id":"js-2-ex1","title":"Grade calculator","lang":"js","starter":"function getGrade(score) {\n  // Your code here\n\n}\n\nconsole.log(getGrade(95));\nconsole.log(getGrade(72));\nconsole.log(getGrade(40));","tests":"test(\"95 is an A\", () => expect(getGrade(95)).toBe(\"A\"));\ntest(\"90 is an A (edge case)\", () => expect(getGrade(90)).toBe(\"A\"));\ntest(\"85 is a B\", () => expect(getGrade(85)).toBe(\"B\"));\ntest(\"70 is a C\", () => expect(getGrade(70)).toBe(\"C\"));\ntest(\"61 is a D\", () => expect(getGrade(61)).toBe(\"D\"));\ntest(\"59 is an F\", () => expect(getGrade(59)).toBe(\"F\"));","solution":"function getGrade(score) {\n  if (score >= 90) {\n    return \"A\";\n  } else if (score >= 80) {\n    return \"B\";\n  } else if (score >= 70) {\n    return \"C\";\n  } else if (score >= 60) {\n    return \"D\";\n  } else {\n    return \"F\";\n  }\n}\n\nconsole.log(getGrade(95));\nconsole.log(getGrade(72));\nconsole.log(getGrade(40));","hints":["<p>Use an <code>if</code> / <code>else if</code> / <code>else</code> chain and check the highest grade first.</p>\n","<p><code>if (score &gt;= 90) { return &quot;A&quot;; } else if (score &gt;= 80) { ... }</code></p>\n"],"html":"<p>Finish <code>getGrade(score)</code> so it returns a letter:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Score</th>\n<th>Grade</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>90 or more</td>\n<td><code>&quot;A&quot;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>80 to 89</td>\n<td><code>&quot;B&quot;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>70 to 79</td>\n<td><code>&quot;C&quot;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>60 to 69</td>\n<td><code>&quot;D&quot;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>below 60</td>\n<td><code>&quot;F&quot;</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n"},{"type":"exercise","id":"js-2-ex2","title":"FizzBuzz","lang":"js","starter":"// Print 1 to 20 here\n","tests":"const expected = [];\nfor (let i = 1; i <= 20; i++) {\n  expected.push(i % 15 === 0 ? \"FizzBuzz\" : i % 3 === 0 ? \"Fizz\" : i % 5 === 0 ? \"Buzz\" : String(i));\n}\ntest(\"prints 20 lines\", () => expect($logs.length).toBe(20));\ntest(\"line 3 is Fizz\", () => expect($logs[2]).toBe(\"Fizz\"));\ntest(\"line 5 is Buzz\", () => expect($logs[4]).toBe(\"Buzz\"));\ntest(\"line 15 is FizzBuzz\", () => expect($logs[14]).toBe(\"FizzBuzz\"));\ntest(\"every line is right\", () => expect($logs).toEqual(expected));","solution":"for (let i = 1; i <= 20; i++) {\n  if (i % 3 === 0 && i % 5 === 0) {\n    console.log(\"FizzBuzz\");\n  } else if (i % 3 === 0) {\n    console.log(\"Fizz\");\n  } else if (i % 5 === 0) {\n    console.log(\"Buzz\");\n  } else {\n    console.log(i);\n  }\n}","hints":["<p><code>i % 3 === 0</code> is true when <code>i</code> is a multiple of 3.</p>\n","<p>Check &quot;both 3 and 5&quot; <strong>first</strong>. Otherwise 15 gets caught by the &quot;multiple of 3&quot; branch.</p>\n"],"html":"<p>A classic. Print the numbers from 1 to 20, one per line, but:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>for multiples of 3 print <code>Fizz</code> instead of the number</li>\n<li>for multiples of 5 print <code>Buzz</code></li>\n<li>for multiples of both 3 and 5 print <code>FizzBuzz</code></li>\n</ul>\n"},{"type":"exercise","id":"js-2-ex3","title":"Add them up","lang":"js","starter":"function sumTo(n) {\n  let total = 0;\n  // Your loop here\n\n  return total;\n}\n\nconsole.log(sumTo(4));","tests":"test(\"sumTo(4) is 10\", () => expect(sumTo(4)).toBe(10));\ntest(\"sumTo(1) is 1\", () => expect(sumTo(1)).toBe(1));\ntest(\"sumTo(100) is 5050\", () => expect(sumTo(100)).toBe(5050));\ntest(\"sumTo(0) is 0\", () => expect(sumTo(0)).toBe(0));","solution":"function sumTo(n) {\n  let total = 0;\n  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {\n    total += i;\n  }\n  return total;\n}\n\nconsole.log(sumTo(4));","hints":["<p><code>for (let i = 1; i &lt;= n; i++) { ... }</code> visits every number from 1 to n.</p>\n","<p>Inside the loop, add <code>i</code> to the running total: <code>total += i;</code></p>\n"],"html":"<p>Finish <code>sumTo(n)</code> so it returns the sum of all whole numbers from 1 to <code>n</code>. For example <code>sumTo(4)</code> is <code>1 + 2 + 3 + 4 = 10</code>. Use a loop.</p>\n"},{"type":"exercise","id":"js-2-ex4","title":"Count the vowels","lang":"js","starter":"function countVowels(text) {\n  // Your code here\n\n}\n\nconsole.log(countVowels(\"Hello World\"));","tests":"test('\"Hello World\" has 3 vowels', () => expect(countVowels(\"Hello World\")).toBe(3));\ntest('\"AEIOU\" has 5 vowels', () => expect(countVowels(\"AEIOU\")).toBe(5));\ntest('\"rhythm\" has 0 vowels', () => expect(countVowels(\"rhythm\")).toBe(0));\ntest('\"\" has 0 vowels', () => expect(countVowels(\"\")).toBe(0));","solution":"function countVowels(text) {\n  let count = 0;\n  for (const letter of text.toLowerCase()) {\n    if (\"aeiou\".includes(letter)) {\n      count++;\n    }\n  }\n  return count;\n}\n\nconsole.log(countVowels(\"Hello World\"));","hints":["<p>Loop over the text with <code>for (const letter of text) { ... }</code> and keep a counter.</p>\n","<p><code>&quot;aeiou&quot;.includes(letter)</code> tells you if a letter is a vowel. Call <code>.toLowerCase()</code> on the text first so capitals count too.</p>\n"],"html":"<p>Finish <code>countVowels(text)</code> so it returns how many vowels (a, e, i, o, u) are in <code>text</code>. Upper and lower case both count.</p>\n"}]},{"id":"js-3","week":1,"session":3,"lang":"js","title":"Functions","minutes":60,"summary":"Package code into reusable functions, pass data in, and get results back.","goals":["Write and call functions with parameters","Understand the difference between <code>return</code> and <code>console.log</code>","Write arrow functions","Know where a variable can be used (scope)","Pass functions to other functions"],"sections":[{"type":"step","title":"Why functions?","html":"<p>A <strong>function</strong> is a named, reusable piece of code. Instead of copying the same lines again and again, you write them once and <strong>call</strong> the function whenever you need it.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>function sayHello() {\n  console.log(&quot;Hello!&quot;);\n  console.log(&quot;Nice to see you.&quot;);\n}\n\nsayHello();\nsayHello();</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>Two separate things happen here:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Declaring</strong> the function: <code>function sayHello() { ... }</code> defines it. Nothing runs yet.</li>\n<li><strong>Calling</strong> it: <code>sayHello()</code> runs the code inside. The brackets <code>()</code> are what make it run.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Writing <code>sayHello</code> without brackets just refers to the function itself without running it. You will use that later in this session.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"Parameters and arguments","html":"<p>Functions get more useful when you pass information in. <strong>Parameters</strong> are the names in the definition; <strong>arguments</strong> are the actual values you pass when you call it.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>function greet(name, timeOfDay) {\n  console.log(`Good ${timeOfDay}, ${name}!`);\n}\n\ngreet(&quot;Ada&quot;, &quot;morning&quot;);\ngreet(&quot;Linus&quot;, &quot;evening&quot;);\ngreet(&quot;Grace&quot;);</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>The last call passed only one argument, so <code>timeOfDay</code> was <code>undefined</code>. JavaScript doesn&#39;t complain about missing arguments, which is another thing TypeScript will check for you later.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"Returning a value","html":"<p><code>return</code> sends a value back to whoever called the function. The call itself then <strong>becomes</strong> that value, so you can store it or use it in a bigger expression.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>function add(a, b) {\n  return a + b;\n}\n\nconst sum = add(2, 3);\nconsole.log(sum);\nconsole.log(add(10, add(1, 1)));</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p><code>return</code> also ends the function immediately. Any code after it in the same block never runs.</p>\n<p>A very common beginner mix-up: <code>console.log</code> only <strong>shows</strong> a value on screen; <code>return</code> <strong>gives it back</strong> to the code. A function that only logs can&#39;t be used in calculations:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>function addAndLog(a, b) {\n  console.log(a + b);\n}\n\nconst result = addAndLog(2, 3);   // prints 5...\nconsole.log(result);              // ...but result is undefined</code></pre></div>\n<p>A function without a <code>return</code> gives back <code>undefined</code>.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"quiz","title":"Quick check","question":"<p>What gets printed by the last line?</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"static\"><pre><code>function double(n) {\n  console.log(n * 2);\n}\nconst x = double(4);\nconsole.log(x);</code></pre></div>\n","options":["<code>8</code>","<code>undefined</code>","<code>null</code>","<code>4</code>"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p><code>double</code> prints 8, but it has no <code>return</code>, so it gives back <code>undefined</code>. That is what <code>x</code> holds.</p>\n"},{"type":"step","title":"Default parameter values","html":"<p>You can give a parameter a default, used when no argument is passed.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>function greet(name, greeting = &quot;Hello&quot;) {\n  return `${greeting}, ${name}!`;\n}\n\nconsole.log(greet(&quot;Ada&quot;));\nconsole.log(greet(&quot;Ada&quot;, &quot;Welcome&quot;));</code></pre></div>\n"},{"type":"step","title":"Arrow functions","html":"<p>There is a shorter way to write functions, called <strong>arrow functions</strong>. You will see these everywhere in modern JavaScript and TypeScript.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>// A normal function declaration\nfunction square(n) {\n  return n * n;\n}\n\n// The same thing as an arrow function stored in a const\nconst squareArrow = (n) =&gt; {\n  return n * n;\n};\n\n// If the body is a single expression, drop the braces and &quot;return&quot;\nconst squareShort = (n) =&gt; n * n;\n\nconsole.log(square(4), squareArrow(4), squareShort(4));</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>The short form returns the expression automatically. This is called an <strong>implicit return</strong>. As soon as you add curly braces, you need to write <code>return</code> yourself.</p>\n<p>More examples:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>const isAdult = (age) =&gt; age &gt;= 18;\nconst fullName = (first, last) =&gt; `${first} ${last}`;\nconst rollDice = () =&gt; Math.floor(Math.random() * 6) + 1;\n\nconsole.log(isAdult(20), fullName(&quot;Ada&quot;, &quot;Lovelace&quot;), rollDice());</code></pre></div>\n<p>Both styles work. Many people use <code>function</code> for main, named functions and arrows for short helpers, especially ones passed to other functions.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"quiz","title":"Quick check","question":"<p>What does <code>const f = (x) =&gt; { x * 2 };</code> return when you call <code>f(5)</code>?</p>\n","options":["<code>10</code>","<code>undefined</code>","<code>5</code>","An error"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>With curly braces there is no implicit return. You need <code>{ return x * 2; }</code>, or drop the braces: <code>(x) =&gt; x * 2</code>.</p>\n"},{"type":"step","title":"Scope: where variables live","html":"<p>A variable created inside a function, or inside any <code>{ }</code> block, only exists <strong>inside</strong> it. That area is its <strong>scope</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>const planet = &quot;Earth&quot;;      // outside any block: usable everywhere below\n\nfunction describe() {\n  const moon = &quot;the Moon&quot;;   // only exists inside describe\n  console.log(`${planet} has ${moon}`);\n}\n\ndescribe();\nconsole.log(planet);\nconsole.log(moon);           // error: moon is not defined out here</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>This is a good thing. It means two functions can each have a variable called <code>total</code> without interfering with each other.</p>\n<p>Blocks such as <code>if</code> and loops create a scope too, when you use <code>let</code> or <code>const</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>if (true) {\n  const secret = 42;\n}\n// secret doesn't exist here</code></pre></div>\n<p>As a rule, keep variables in the smallest scope that works.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"Functions are values","html":"<p>In JavaScript, a function is a value like a number or a string. You can store it in a variable and <strong>pass it to another function</strong>. This idea powers a lot of JavaScript, including next week&#39;s array methods.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>function repeat(times, action) {\n  for (let i = 1; i &lt;= times; i++) {\n    action(i);\n  }\n}\n\nrepeat(3, (n) =&gt; console.log(`Round ${n}`));\n\nconst shout = (n) =&gt; console.log(`ROUND ${n}!!!`);\nrepeat(2, shout);</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>A function that you pass into another function is often called a <strong>callback</strong>. Notice we passed <code>shout</code> without brackets: we hand over the function itself, and <code>repeat</code> decides when to call it.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"Closures: functions that remember","html":"<p>A function created inside another function can use the outer function&#39;s variables, and keeps access to them <strong>even after the outer function has finished</strong>. This is called a <strong>closure</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>function makeCounter() {\n  let count = 0;\n  return () =&gt; {\n    count++;\n    return count;\n  };\n}\n\nconst counter = makeCounter();\nconsole.log(counter());\nconsole.log(counter());\nconsole.log(counter());\n\nconst other = makeCounter();   // a brand-new, separate count\nconsole.log(other());</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>Each call to <code>makeCounter</code> creates a fresh <code>count</code> variable. The returned arrow function keeps a private link to it. Nothing else can touch <code>count</code> directly, so closures are one way to hide data.</p>\n<p>Don&#39;t worry if this feels abstract. You will recognise the pattern when you see it, and that is enough for now.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"exercise","id":"js-3-ex1","title":"Two small helpers","lang":"js","starter":"// const square = ...\n\n// const isEven = ...\n\nconsole.log(square(5));\nconsole.log(isEven(4), isEven(7));","tests":"test(\"square(5) is 25\", () => expect(square(5)).toBe(25));\ntest(\"square(-3) is 9\", () => expect(square(-3)).toBe(9));\ntest(\"isEven(4) is true\", () => expect(isEven(4)).toBe(true));\ntest(\"isEven(7) is false\", () => expect(isEven(7)).toBe(false));\ntest(\"isEven(0) is true\", () => expect(isEven(0)).toBe(true));","solution":"const square = (n) => n * n;\n\nconst isEven = (n) => n % 2 === 0;\n\nconsole.log(square(5));\nconsole.log(isEven(4), isEven(7));","hints":["<p><code>const square = (n) =&gt; n * n;</code></p>\n","<p>A number is even when the remainder after dividing by 2 is 0. A comparison already gives <code>true</code> or <code>false</code>, so you can return it directly.</p>\n"],"html":"<p>Write two arrow functions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>square</code> takes a number and returns it multiplied by itself</li>\n<li><code>isEven</code> takes a number and returns <code>true</code> if it is even, <code>false</code> otherwise</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"type":"exercise","id":"js-3-ex2","title":"Greeting with a default","lang":"js","starter":"function greet(name, greeting) {\n  // Your code here\n\n}\n\nconsole.log(greet(\"Ada\"));\nconsole.log(greet(\"Linus\", \"Hi\"));","tests":"test('greet(\"Ada\") returns \"Hello, Ada!\"', () => expect(greet(\"Ada\")).toBe(\"Hello, Ada!\"));\ntest('greet(\"Linus\", \"Hi\") returns \"Hi, Linus!\"', () => expect(greet(\"Linus\", \"Hi\")).toBe(\"Hi, Linus!\"));","solution":"function greet(name, greeting = \"Hello\") {\n  return `${greeting}, ${name}!`;\n}\n\nconsole.log(greet(\"Ada\"));\nconsole.log(greet(\"Linus\", \"Hi\"));","hints":["<p>Give the parameter a default: <code>function greet(name, greeting = &quot;Hello&quot;)</code>.</p>\n","<p>Use <code>return</code> with a template literal: <code>return `${greeting}, ${name}!`;</code></p>\n"],"html":"<p>Write a function <code>greet(name, greeting)</code> that returns (not prints) a string like <code>&quot;Hello, Ada!&quot;</code>. If no greeting is passed, use <code>&quot;Hello&quot;</code>.</p>\n"},{"type":"exercise","id":"js-3-ex3","title":"Apply it twice","lang":"js","starter":"function applyTwice(fn, value) {\n  // Your code here\n\n}\n\nconst addThree = (n) => n + 3;\nconsole.log(applyTwice(addThree, 10));\nconsole.log(applyTwice((s) => s + \"!\", \"Hi\"));","tests":"test(\"applyTwice(addThree, 10) is 16\", () => expect(applyTwice((n) => n + 3, 10)).toBe(16));\ntest(\"works with strings\", () => expect(applyTwice((s) => s + \"!\", \"Hi\")).toBe(\"Hi!!\"));\ntest(\"works with doubling\", () => expect(applyTwice((n) => n * 2, 5)).toBe(20));","solution":"function applyTwice(fn, value) {\n  return fn(fn(value));\n}\n\nconst addThree = (n) => n + 3;\nconsole.log(applyTwice(addThree, 10));\nconsole.log(applyTwice((s) => s + \"!\", \"Hi\"));","hints":["<p><code>fn(value)</code> calls the function once. What happens if you pass that result into <code>fn</code> again?</p>\n"],"html":"<p>Write <code>applyTwice(fn, value)</code>. It takes a function and a value, calls the function on the value, then calls the function <strong>again on the result</strong>, and returns that final result.</p>\n<p>For example, with <code>addThree = (n) =&gt; n + 3</code>, <code>applyTwice(addThree, 10)</code> is <code>16</code>.</p>\n"},{"type":"exercise","id":"js-3-ex4","title":"A counter with a closure","lang":"js","starter":"function makeCounter(start) {\n  // Your code here\n\n}\n\nconst next = makeCounter(5);\nconsole.log(next(), next(), next());","tests":"test(\"first call returns the start value\", () => expect(makeCounter(5)()).toBe(5));\ntest(\"counts up by one each call\", () => {\n  const c = makeCounter(10);\n  c();\n  c();\n  expect(c()).toBe(12);\n});\ntest(\"counters are independent\", () => {\n  const a = makeCounter(0);\n  const b = makeCounter(100);\n  a(); a();\n  expect(b()).toBe(100);\n  expect(a()).toBe(2);\n});","solution":"function makeCounter(start) {\n  let current = start;\n  return () => {\n    const value = current;\n    current++;\n    return value;\n  };\n}\n\nconst next = makeCounter(5);\nconsole.log(next(), next(), next());","hints":["<p>Create a <code>let</code> variable inside <code>makeCounter</code>, then <code>return</code> an arrow function that uses and changes it.</p>\n","<p>The first call must return <code>start</code> itself, so remember the current value before you add 1.</p>\n"],"html":"<p>Write <code>makeCounter(start)</code>. It returns a function. Each time that function is called, it returns the next number, beginning with <code>start</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"static\"><pre><code>const next = makeCounter(5);\nnext(); // 5\nnext(); // 6\nnext(); // 7</code></pre></div>\n"}]},{"id":"js-4","week":2,"session":1,"lang":"js","title":"Arrays","minutes":60,"summary":"Keep lists of values in arrays and transform them with <code>map</code>, <code>filter</code> and <code>reduce</code>.","goals":["Create arrays and read items by index","Add, remove and find items","Transform lists with <code>map</code>, <code>filter</code>, <code>find</code> and <code>reduce</code>","Sort and copy arrays safely"],"sections":[{"type":"step","title":"Making a list","html":"<p>An <strong>array</strong> is an ordered list of values, written in square brackets. Each item has a position called its <strong>index</strong>, starting at <code>0</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>const fruits = [&quot;apple&quot;, &quot;banana&quot;, &quot;cherry&quot;];\n\nconsole.log(fruits);\nconsole.log(fruits[0]);              // first item\nconsole.log(fruits[2]);              // third item\nconsole.log(fruits.length);          // how many items\nconsole.log(fruits[fruits.length - 1]);  // last item\nconsole.log(fruits[10]);             // doesn't exist</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>Arrays can hold any type, even a mix (<code>[1, &quot;two&quot;, true]</code>), though in practice you usually keep one type per array. TypeScript will help enforce that in week 3.</p>\n<p>Reading an index that doesn&#39;t exist gives <code>undefined</code>, not an error. You can also use <code>fruits.at(-1)</code> to get the last item.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"Changing an array","html":"<p>Arrays can grow and shrink, even when stored in a <code>const</code>. The <code>const</code> stops you replacing the whole array with a new one, not changing what&#39;s inside it.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>const queue = [&quot;Ada&quot;, &quot;Grace&quot;];\n\nqueue.push(&quot;Linus&quot;);      // add to the end\nconsole.log(queue);\n\nqueue.unshift(&quot;Alan&quot;);    // add to the start\nconsole.log(queue);\n\nconst last = queue.pop(); // remove from the end\nconsole.log(last, queue);\n\nconst first = queue.shift(); // remove from the start\nconsole.log(first, queue);\n\nqueue[0] = &quot;Margaret&quot;;    // replace an item\nconsole.log(queue);</code></pre></div>\n"},{"type":"step","title":"Searching an array","html":"<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>const colors = [&quot;red&quot;, &quot;green&quot;, &quot;blue&quot;];\n\nconsole.log(colors.includes(&quot;green&quot;));  // is it there?\nconsole.log(colors.indexOf(&quot;blue&quot;));    // where is it?\nconsole.log(colors.indexOf(&quot;pink&quot;));    // -1 means &quot;not found&quot;\nconsole.log(colors.join(&quot; / &quot;));        // glue into one string\nconsole.log(&quot;a,b,c&quot;.split(&quot;,&quot;));        // split a string into an array</code></pre></div>\n"},{"type":"step","title":"Looping over an array","html":"<p><code>for...of</code> gives you each item in turn. If you need the position too, <code>forEach</code> passes both.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>const scores = [12, 7, 21];\n\nfor (const score of scores) {\n  console.log(score);\n}\n\nscores.forEach((score, index) =&gt; {\n  console.log(`#${index + 1}: ${score}`);\n});</code></pre></div>\n"},{"type":"step","title":"map: transform every item","html":"<p><code>map</code> calls your function on every item and builds a <strong>new array</strong> from what it returns. The original array is unchanged.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>const prices = [10, 25, 4];\n\nconst withTax = prices.map((price) =&gt; price * 1.2);\nconst labels = prices.map((price) =&gt; `$${price}`);\n\nconsole.log(withTax);\nconsole.log(labels);\nconsole.log(prices);   // untouched</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>Same length in, same length out: <code>map</code> always gives back exactly one new item for each old item. Use it whenever you think &quot;turn each X into a Y&quot;.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"filter: keep some items","html":"<p><code>filter</code> calls your function on every item and keeps the items where it returns <code>true</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>const ages = [12, 30, 17, 45, 8];\n\nconst adults = ages.filter((age) =&gt; age &gt;= 18);\nconst kids = ages.filter((age) =&gt; age &lt; 18);\n\nconsole.log(adults);\nconsole.log(kids);</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>You can chain these methods, because each one returns a new array:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>const words = [&quot;tree&quot;, &quot;a&quot;, &quot;elephant&quot;, &quot;to&quot;, &quot;garden&quot;];\n\nconst result = words\n  .filter((w) =&gt; w.length &gt; 3)\n  .map((w) =&gt; w.toUpperCase());\n\nconsole.log(result);</code></pre></div>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"find, some and every","html":"<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>const numbers = [3, 8, 12, 5];\n\nconsole.log(numbers.find((n) =&gt; n &gt; 6));      // first match\nconsole.log(numbers.find((n) =&gt; n &gt; 100));    // undefined: none found\nconsole.log(numbers.some((n) =&gt; n &gt; 10));     // at least one?\nconsole.log(numbers.every((n) =&gt; n &gt; 0));     // all of them?\nconsole.log(numbers.findIndex((n) =&gt; n === 12));</code></pre></div>\n"},{"type":"step","title":"reduce: boil a list down to one value","html":"<p><code>reduce</code> walks through the array carrying a running result (the <strong>accumulator</strong>). You give it a function and a starting value.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>const cart = [5, 12, 3];\n\nconst total = cart.reduce((sum, price) =&gt; sum + price, 0);\nconsole.log(total);</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>Step by step, with a starting value of <code>0</code>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Round</th>\n<th>sum</th>\n<th>price</th>\n<th>returns</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>12</td>\n<td>17</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>17</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>20</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><code>reduce</code> is the most flexible of the array methods, and the hardest to read. When a simple loop or <code>map</code>/<code>filter</code> does the job, prefer those.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>const biggest = [4, 19, 7].reduce((max, n) =&gt; (n &gt; max ? n : max), -Infinity);\nconsole.log(biggest);\nconsole.log(Math.max(...[4, 19, 7]));   // an easier way for this one</code></pre></div>\n</div>"},{"type":"quiz","title":"Quick check","question":"<p><code>[1, 2, 3, 4].filter((n) =&gt; n &gt; 2)</code> gives you...</p>\n","options":["<code>[1, 2]</code>","<code>[3, 4]</code>","<code>[false, false, true, true]</code>","<code>2</code>"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p><code>filter</code> keeps the items for which the function returns <code>true</code>. <code>map</code> would give you the list of booleans.</p>\n"},{"type":"step","title":"Sorting","html":"<p><code>sort</code> reorders an array <strong>in place</strong>, changing the original. Careful: by default it sorts as <strong>text</strong>, even for numbers.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>const names = [&quot;Tom&quot;, &quot;ann&quot;, &quot;Bea&quot;];\nconsole.log([...names].sort());\n\nconst nums = [10, 1, 5, 100];\nconsole.log([...nums].sort());                  // text order: wrong for numbers\nconsole.log([...nums].sort((a, b) =&gt; a - b));   // smallest first\nconsole.log([...nums].sort((a, b) =&gt; b - a));   // biggest first</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>The compare function returns a negative number if <code>a</code> should come first, positive if <code>b</code> should, and <code>0</code> if they&#39;re equal. <code>a - b</code> does exactly that for numbers.</p>\n<p>The <code>[...nums]</code> part makes a copy first, so the original isn&#39;t changed. That&#39;s the spread syntax, coming next. Newer JavaScript also has <code>toSorted()</code>, which returns a sorted copy directly.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"Spread, copy and destructure","html":"<p>The <strong>spread</strong> syntax <code>...</code> unpacks an array&#39;s items. It&#39;s the easiest way to copy or combine arrays.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>const a = [1, 2];\nconst b = [3, 4];\n\nconst both = [...a, ...b, 5];\nconst copy = [...a];\ncopy.push(99);\n\nconsole.log(both);\nconsole.log(a, copy);    // a is unchanged\n\nconsole.log([1, 2, 3, 4, 5].slice(1, 3));  // a copy of part of an array\n\n// Destructuring: pull items out into variables\nconst [first, second, ...rest] = [&quot;gold&quot;, &quot;silver&quot;, &quot;bronze&quot;, &quot;fourth&quot;];\nconsole.log(first, second, rest);</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>Why copy at all? Because arrays are passed around by <strong>reference</strong>: two variables can point at the very same array. Change it through one, and the other sees the change:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>const original = [1, 2, 3];\nconst sameArray = original;\nsameArray.push(4);\nconsole.log(original);   // [1, 2, 3, 4]: surprise!</code></pre></div>\n<p>Copying with <code>[...original]</code> avoids that. You&#39;ll see the same idea with objects next session.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"quiz","title":"Quick check","question":"<p>What does <code>[10, 9, 1].sort()</code> give?</p>\n","options":["<code>[1, 9, 10]</code>","<code>[1, 10, 9]</code>","<code>[10, 9, 1]</code>","<code>[9, 10, 1]</code>"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Without a compare function, <code>sort</code> compares items as text. <code>&quot;10&quot;</code> comes before <code>&quot;9&quot;</code> because <code>&quot;1&quot;</code> comes before <code>&quot;9&quot;</code>. Use <code>.sort((a, b) =&gt; a - b)</code> for numbers.</p>\n"},{"type":"exercise","id":"js-4-ex1","title":"Double everything","lang":"js","starter":"function doubleAll(numbers) {\n  // Your code here\n\n}\n\nconsole.log(doubleAll([1, 2, 3]));","tests":"test(\"doubles [1, 2, 3]\", () => expect(doubleAll([1, 2, 3])).toEqual([2, 4, 6]));\ntest(\"works on an empty array\", () => expect(doubleAll([])).toEqual([]));\ntest(\"doesn't change the original\", () => {\n  const input = [5, 6];\n  doubleAll(input);\n  expect(input).toEqual([5, 6]);\n});","solution":"function doubleAll(numbers) {\n  return numbers.map((n) => n * 2);\n}\n\nconsole.log(doubleAll([1, 2, 3]));","hints":["<p><code>return numbers.map((n) =&gt; ...);</code></p>\n"],"html":"<p>Write <code>doubleAll(numbers)</code> that returns a <strong>new</strong> array with every number doubled. Use <code>map</code>.</p>\n"},{"type":"exercise","id":"js-4-ex2","title":"Long words only","lang":"js","starter":"function longWords(words, minLength) {\n  // Your code here\n\n}\n\nconsole.log(longWords([\"sun\", \"planet\", \"galaxy\", \"moon\"], 5));","tests":"test(\"keeps words with 5+ letters\", () =>\n  expect(longWords([\"sun\", \"planet\", \"galaxy\", \"moon\"], 5)).toEqual([\"PLANET\", \"GALAXY\"]));\ntest(\"minLength is inclusive\", () => expect(longWords([\"abc\", \"ab\"], 3)).toEqual([\"ABC\"]));\ntest(\"returns [] when nothing matches\", () => expect(longWords([\"a\", \"b\"], 4)).toEqual([]));","solution":"function longWords(words, minLength) {\n  return words\n    .filter((word) => word.length >= minLength)\n    .map((word) => word.toUpperCase());\n}\n\nconsole.log(longWords([\"sun\", \"planet\", \"galaxy\", \"moon\"], 5));","hints":["<p>First <code>filter</code> by <code>word.length &gt;= minLength</code>, then <code>map</code> to upper case.</p>\n"],"html":"<p>Write <code>longWords(words, minLength)</code> that returns only the words with at least <code>minLength</code> characters, in <strong>upper case</strong>.</p>\n"},{"type":"exercise","id":"js-4-ex3","title":"Average score","lang":"js","starter":"function average(numbers) {\n  // Your code here\n\n}\n\nconsole.log(average([4, 8, 6]));","tests":"test(\"average of [4, 8, 6] is 6\", () => expect(average([4, 8, 6])).toBe(6));\ntest(\"average of [1, 2] is 1.5\", () => expect(average([1, 2])).toBe(1.5));\ntest(\"average of [] is 0\", () => expect(average([])).toBe(0));","solution":"function average(numbers) {\n  if (numbers.length === 0) {\n    return 0;\n  }\n  const total = numbers.reduce((sum, n) => sum + n, 0);\n  return total / numbers.length;\n}\n\nconsole.log(average([4, 8, 6]));","hints":["<p>Get the total with <code>reduce</code> (or a loop), then divide by <code>numbers.length</code>.</p>\n","<p>Handle the empty array first: <code>if (numbers.length === 0) return 0;</code>. Dividing by zero would give <code>NaN</code>.</p>\n"],"html":"<p>Write <code>average(numbers)</code> that returns the average (the total divided by how many there are). An empty array should return <code>0</code>.</p>\n"},{"type":"exercise","id":"js-4-ex4","title":"Top scores","lang":"js","starter":"function topScores(scores, n) {\n  // Your code here\n\n}\n\nconst scores = [40, 95, 72, 88, 61];\nconsole.log(topScores(scores, 3));\nconsole.log(scores);","tests":"test(\"top 3 of [40, 95, 72, 88, 61]\", () => expect(topScores([40, 95, 72, 88, 61], 3)).toEqual([95, 88, 72]));\ntest(\"sorts numerically, not as text\", () => expect(topScores([9, 100, 20], 2)).toEqual([100, 20]));\ntest(\"doesn't change the original\", () => {\n  const input = [1, 3, 2];\n  topScores(input, 2);\n  expect(input).toEqual([1, 3, 2]);\n});","solution":"function topScores(scores, n) {\n  return [...scores].sort((a, b) => b - a).slice(0, n);\n}\n\nconst scores = [40, 95, 72, 88, 61];\nconsole.log(topScores(scores, 3));\nconsole.log(scores);","hints":["<p>Copy first with <code>[...scores]</code>, then sort with a compare function.</p>\n","<p><code>.sort((a, b) =&gt; b - a)</code> puts the biggest first. <code>.slice(0, n)</code> keeps the first <code>n</code>.</p>\n"],"html":"<p>Write <code>topScores(scores, n)</code> that returns the <code>n</code> highest scores, biggest first. It must <strong>not</strong> change the original array.</p>\n"}]},{"id":"js-5","week":2,"session":2,"lang":"js","title":"Objects","minutes":60,"summary":"Group related data into objects, work with lists of objects, and copy them safely.","goals":["Create objects and read, add and change properties","Write methods and use <code>this</code>","Work with arrays of objects","Use destructuring, spread and <code>Object.entries</code>","Convert data to and from JSON"],"sections":[{"type":"step","title":"What an object is","html":"<p>An array is a list in order. An <strong>object</strong> is a set of named values, written in curly braces. Each entry is a <strong>property</strong>: a key, a colon, and a value.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>const book = {\n  title: &quot;Dune&quot;,\n  author: &quot;Frank Herbert&quot;,\n  pages: 412,\n  isRead: false,\n};\n\nconsole.log(book);\nconsole.log(book.title);\nconsole.log(book[&quot;pages&quot;]);</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>Use <strong>dot notation</strong> (<code>book.title</code>) most of the time. Use <strong>brackets</strong> (<code>book[&quot;pages&quot;]</code>) when the key is in a variable or isn&#39;t a simple word:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>const user = { name: &quot;Ada&quot;, &quot;favourite color&quot;: &quot;green&quot; };\nconst field = &quot;name&quot;;\n\nconsole.log(user[field]);\nconsole.log(user[&quot;favourite color&quot;]);</code></pre></div>\n<p>Objects are how almost all real data is shaped: a user, a product, a message, a settings file. In TypeScript you will describe these shapes precisely.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"Adding, changing and removing","html":"<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>const user = { name: &quot;Grace&quot;, age: 30 };\n\nuser.age = 31;               // change\nuser.email = &quot;g@navy.mil&quot;;   // add a new property\ndelete user.name;            // remove\n\nconsole.log(user);\nconsole.log(user.phone);     // missing properties are undefined\nconsole.log(&quot;email&quot; in user);</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>Like arrays, an object stored in a <code>const</code> can still be changed inside. <code>const</code> only stops you pointing the variable at a different object.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"Nesting","html":"<p>Values can be anything, including arrays and other objects.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>const order = {\n  id: 1042,\n  customer: { name: &quot;Linus&quot;, city: &quot;Helsinki&quot; },\n  items: [&quot;keyboard&quot;, &quot;mouse&quot;],\n};\n\nconsole.log(order.customer.city);\nconsole.log(order.items[1]);\nconsole.log(order.items.length);</code></pre></div>\n"},{"type":"step","title":"Methods and this","html":"<p>A function stored in an object is called a <strong>method</strong>. Inside a method, <code>this</code> refers to the object it was called on.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>const counter = {\n  count: 0,\n  increment() {\n    this.count++;\n    return this.count;\n  },\n  describe() {\n    return `Count is ${this.count}`;\n  },\n};\n\ncounter.increment();\ncounter.increment();\nconsole.log(counter.describe());</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p><code>increment() { ... }</code> is shorthand for <code>increment: function () { ... }</code>.</p>\n<p>Watch out: arrow functions don&#39;t get their own <code>this</code>, so don&#39;t use arrows for methods that need <code>this</code>. You&#39;ll lean on <code>this</code> a lot more when you meet classes in week 4.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"Arrays of objects","html":"<p>This is the shape of most real-world data: a list where each item is an object. Everything you learned about <code>map</code>, <code>filter</code> and <code>reduce</code> applies.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>const products = [\n  { name: &quot;Lamp&quot;, price: 30, inStock: true },\n  { name: &quot;Desk&quot;, price: 150, inStock: false },\n  { name: &quot;Chair&quot;, price: 85, inStock: true },\n];\n\nconst available = products.filter((p) =&gt; p.inStock);\nconst names = available.map((p) =&gt; p.name);\nconst totalValue = products.reduce((sum, p) =&gt; sum + p.price, 0);\nconst desk = products.find((p) =&gt; p.name === &quot;Desk&quot;);\n\nconsole.log(names);\nconsole.log(totalValue);\nconsole.log(desk);</code></pre></div>\n"},{"type":"quiz","title":"Quick check","question":"<p>Given <code>const p = { name: &quot;Ada&quot;, langs: [&quot;js&quot;, &quot;ts&quot;] };</code>, how do you get <code>&quot;ts&quot;</code>?</p>\n","options":["<code>p.langs.ts</code>","<code>p[langs][1]</code>","<code>p.langs[1]</code>","<code>p.langs(1)</code>"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p><code>p.langs</code> is the array, and <code>[1]</code> gets its second item.</p>\n"},{"type":"step","title":"Destructuring and shorthand","html":"<p><strong>Destructuring</strong> pulls properties out into variables in one line. <strong>Shorthand</strong> does the reverse: if a variable has the same name as the key, you can write it once.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>const movie = { title: &quot;Alien&quot;, year: 1979, rating: 8.5 };\n\nconst { title, year } = movie;\nconsole.log(title, year);\n\n// rename while destructuring, and give a default\nconst { rating: score, genre = &quot;unknown&quot; } = movie;\nconsole.log(score, genre);\n\n// shorthand: { name: name, age: age } becomes { name, age }\nconst name = &quot;Ada&quot;;\nconst age = 36;\nconst person = { name, age };\nconsole.log(person);\n\n// destructuring works in function parameters too\nfunction describe({ title, year }) {\n  return `${title} (${year})`;\n}\nconsole.log(describe(movie));</code></pre></div>\n"},{"type":"step","title":"Spread: copying and merging","html":"<p><code>...</code> works with objects too. Later properties win.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>const defaults = { theme: &quot;light&quot;, fontSize: 14, sound: true };\nconst userSettings = { theme: &quot;dark&quot; };\n\nconst settings = { ...defaults, ...userSettings };\nconsole.log(settings);\n\nconst updated = { ...settings, fontSize: 16 };\nconsole.log(updated);\nconsole.log(settings.fontSize);   // the original is unchanged</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>Objects are passed by <strong>reference</strong>, just like arrays. Assigning an object to another variable doesn&#39;t copy it:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>const a = { score: 1 };\nconst b = a;\nb.score = 99;\nconsole.log(a.score);   // 99\n\nconst c = { ...a };     // a real (shallow) copy\nc.score = 5;\nconsole.log(a.score);   // still 99</code></pre></div>\n<p>This is also why <code>{ x: 1 } === { x: 1 }</code> is <code>false</code>: they&#39;re two different objects, even though they look the same.</p>\n<p>The spread copy is <strong>shallow</strong>: nested objects inside are still shared. For a full deep copy use <code>structuredClone(obj)</code>.</p>\n<p>Building new objects instead of changing old ones (<code>{ ...user, age: 31 }</code>) is a very common style in modern JavaScript and TypeScript, especially in React.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"Looping over an object","html":"<p><code>Object.keys</code>, <code>Object.values</code> and <code>Object.entries</code> turn an object into arrays, so you can loop over it.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>const stock = { apples: 5, pears: 0, plums: 12 };\n\nconsole.log(Object.keys(stock));\nconsole.log(Object.values(stock));\nconsole.log(Object.entries(stock));\n\nfor (const [fruit, count] of Object.entries(stock)) {\n  console.log(`${fruit}: ${count}`);\n}</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>Objects also make great <strong>lookup tables</strong> and <strong>counters</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>const words = [&quot;cat&quot;, &quot;dog&quot;, &quot;cat&quot;, &quot;bird&quot;, &quot;cat&quot;];\nconst counts = {};\n\nfor (const word of words) {\n  counts[word] = (counts[word] || 0) + 1;\n}\nconsole.log(counts);</code></pre></div>\n<p>If the key isn&#39;t there yet, <code>counts[word]</code> is <code>undefined</code>, so <code>|| 0</code> gives a starting value of 0.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"JSON","html":"<p><strong>JSON</strong> (JavaScript Object Notation) is the text format used to send data between programs and over the internet. It looks almost exactly like JavaScript objects, but keys must be in double quotes.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>const user = { name: &quot;Ada&quot;, skills: [&quot;maths&quot;, &quot;code&quot;] };\n\nconst text = JSON.stringify(user);\nconsole.log(text);\nconsole.log(typeof text);\n\nconst back = JSON.parse('{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;Linus&quot;,&quot;age&quot;:28}');\nconsole.log(back.name, back.age);\n\nconsole.log(JSON.stringify(user, null, 2));   // pretty printed</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>When you fetch data from a web API, it arrives as JSON text and you <code>JSON.parse</code> it into objects. JavaScript has no idea what shape that data has. TypeScript lets you describe it, which is one of its most useful features.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"quiz","title":"Quick check","question":"<p>What does this print?</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"static\"><pre><code>const a = { n: 1 };\nconst b = a;\nb.n = 2;\nconsole.log(a.n);</code></pre></div>\n","options":["<code>1</code>","<code>2</code>","<code>undefined</code>","An error"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p><code>b</code> and <code>a</code> point at the same object, so changing <code>b.n</code> changes what <code>a</code> sees too.</p>\n"},{"type":"exercise","id":"js-5-ex1","title":"Make a book","lang":"js","starter":"function createBook(title, author, pages) {\n  // Your code here\n\n}\n\nconsole.log(createBook(\"Dune\", \"Frank Herbert\", 412));","tests":"test(\"has the right properties\", () =>\n  expect(createBook(\"Dune\", \"Frank Herbert\", 412)).toEqual({ title: \"Dune\", author: \"Frank Herbert\", pages: 412, isLong: true }));\ntest(\"a short book is not long\", () => expect(createBook(\"Short\", \"Me\", 120).isLong).toBe(false));\ntest(\"exactly 300 pages is not long\", () => expect(createBook(\"Edge\", \"Me\", 300).isLong).toBe(false));","solution":"function createBook(title, author, pages) {\n  return {\n    title,\n    author,\n    pages,\n    isLong: pages > 300,\n  };\n}\n\nconsole.log(createBook(\"Dune\", \"Frank Herbert\", 412));","hints":["<p>Return an object literal: <code>return { title: title, ... };</code>. With shorthand you can write just <code>title,</code>.</p>\n","<p><code>isLong: pages &gt; 300</code> stores the result of the comparison.</p>\n"],"html":"<p>Write <code>createBook(title, author, pages)</code> that returns an object with the properties <code>title</code>, <code>author</code>, <code>pages</code>, and <code>isLong</code>, which is <code>true</code> when the book has more than 300 pages.</p>\n"},{"type":"exercise","id":"js-5-ex2","title":"Titles of unread books","lang":"js","starter":"function unreadTitles(books) {\n  // Your code here\n\n}\n\nconst shelf = [\n  { title: \"Dune\", isRead: true },\n  { title: \"Emma\", isRead: false },\n  { title: \"Ulysses\", isRead: false },\n];\nconsole.log(unreadTitles(shelf));","tests":"const shelf2 = [\n  { title: \"Dune\", isRead: true },\n  { title: \"Emma\", isRead: false },\n  { title: \"Ulysses\", isRead: false },\n];\ntest(\"returns the unread titles\", () => expect(unreadTitles(shelf2)).toEqual([\"Emma\", \"Ulysses\"]));\ntest(\"returns [] when all are read\", () => expect(unreadTitles([{ title: \"A\", isRead: true }])).toEqual([]));","solution":"function unreadTitles(books) {\n  return books.filter((book) => !book.isRead).map((book) => book.title);\n}\n\nconst shelf = [\n  { title: \"Dune\", isRead: true },\n  { title: \"Emma\", isRead: false },\n  { title: \"Ulysses\", isRead: false },\n];\nconsole.log(unreadTitles(shelf));","hints":["<p><code>filter</code> for the unread ones, then <code>map</code> each book to its title.</p>\n"],"html":"<p>Write <code>unreadTitles(books)</code> that returns an array with the titles of the books where <code>isRead</code> is <code>false</code>.</p>\n"},{"type":"exercise","id":"js-5-ex3","title":"Word counter","lang":"js","starter":"function countWords(text) {\n  const counts = {};\n  // Your code here\n\n  return counts;\n}\n\nconsole.log(countWords(\"the cat and the hat\"));","tests":"test(\"counts each word\", () => expect(countWords(\"the cat and the hat\")).toEqual({ the: 2, cat: 1, and: 1, hat: 1 }));\ntest(\"ignores case\", () => expect(countWords(\"Go go GO\")).toEqual({ go: 3 }));","solution":"function countWords(text) {\n  const counts = {};\n  for (const word of text.toLowerCase().split(\" \")) {\n    counts[word] = (counts[word] || 0) + 1;\n  }\n  return counts;\n}\n\nconsole.log(countWords(\"the cat and the hat\"));","hints":["<p><code>text.toLowerCase().split(&quot; &quot;)</code> gives you an array of words.</p>\n","<p>For each word: <code>counts[word] = (counts[word] || 0) + 1;</code></p>\n"],"html":"<p>Write <code>countWords(text)</code> that returns an object counting how many times each word appears. Treat upper and lower case the same, and split on spaces.</p>\n<p><code>countWords(&quot;the cat and the hat&quot;)</code> should give <code>{ the: 2, cat: 1, and: 1, hat: 1 }</code>.</p>\n"},{"type":"exercise","id":"js-5-ex4","title":"Update without changing","lang":"js","starter":"function updateUser(user, changes) {\n  // Your code here\n\n}\n\nconst ada = { name: \"Ada\", age: 36, city: \"London\" };\nconst older = updateUser(ada, { age: 37 });\nconsole.log(older);\nconsole.log(ada);","tests":"const original = { name: \"Ada\", age: 36, city: \"London\" };\ntest(\"applies the changes\", () =>\n  expect(updateUser(original, { age: 37 })).toEqual({ name: \"Ada\", age: 37, city: \"London\" }));\ntest(\"can add new properties\", () =>\n  expect(updateUser({ a: 1 }, { b: 2 })).toEqual({ a: 1, b: 2 }));\ntest(\"doesn't change the original\", () => {\n  updateUser(original, { city: \"Paris\" });\n  expect(original.city).toBe(\"London\");\n});\ntest(\"returns a different object\", () => {\n  const result = updateUser(original, {});\n  expect(result === original).toBe(false);\n});","solution":"function updateUser(user, changes) {\n  return { ...user, ...changes };\n}\n\nconst ada = { name: \"Ada\", age: 36, city: \"London\" };\nconst older = updateUser(ada, { age: 37 });\nconsole.log(older);\nconsole.log(ada);","hints":["<p>Spread both objects into a new one. Which one should come last so its values win?</p>\n"],"html":"<p>Write <code>updateUser(user, changes)</code> that returns a <strong>new</strong> object with the changes applied. The original <code>user</code> must not change.</p>\n"}]},{"id":"js-6","week":2,"session":3,"lang":"js","title":"Errors and async code","minutes":60,"summary":"Handle errors with <code>try</code>/<code>catch</code>, and write code that waits for slow things with Promises and <code>async</code>/<code>await</code>.","goals":["Catch and throw errors","Understand why some code runs &quot;later&quot;","Create and use Promises","Write <code>async</code> functions with <code>await</code>","Run several tasks at once with <code>Promise.all</code>"],"sections":[{"type":"step","title":"When things go wrong","html":"<p>When JavaScript hits a problem it <strong>throws an error</strong> and stops. You can catch errors with <code>try</code> / <code>catch</code> so your program can recover.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>try {\n  const data = JSON.parse(&quot;this is not JSON&quot;);\n  console.log(data);\n} catch (error) {\n  console.log(&quot;Could not read the data:&quot;, error.message);\n}\n\nconsole.log(&quot;The program keeps going&quot;);</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><ul>\n<li>Code in <code>try { }</code> runs normally.</li>\n<li>If anything inside throws, JavaScript jumps straight to <code>catch (error) { }</code>.</li>\n<li><code>error.message</code> holds a human-readable description.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>There&#39;s also an optional <code>finally { }</code> block, which runs whether or not there was an error. It&#39;s useful for clean-up, like hiding a loading spinner.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"Throwing your own errors","html":"<p>Use <code>throw new Error(&quot;message&quot;)</code> when your function gets input it can&#39;t handle. It&#39;s better to fail loudly than to quietly return a wrong answer.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>function withdraw(balance, amount) {\n  if (amount &gt; balance) {\n    throw new Error(&quot;Not enough money&quot;);\n  }\n  return balance - amount;\n}\n\nconsole.log(withdraw(100, 30));\n\ntry {\n  console.log(withdraw(100, 500));\n} catch (error) {\n  console.log(&quot;Failed:&quot;, error.message);\n} finally {\n  console.log(&quot;Transaction finished&quot;);\n}</code></pre></div>\n"},{"type":"step","title":"Code that runs later","html":"<p>Some things take time: loading data from the internet, reading a file, waiting for a timer. JavaScript doesn&#39;t stop and wait for them. It carries on, and runs your code <strong>later</strong> when the slow thing is done. This is <strong>asynchronous</strong> code.</p>\n<p><code>setTimeout(fn, ms)</code> is the simplest example: run <code>fn</code> after <code>ms</code> milliseconds.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>console.log(&quot;1. Order coffee&quot;);\n\nsetTimeout(() =&gt; {\n  console.log(&quot;3. Coffee is ready&quot;);\n}, 1000);\n\nconsole.log(&quot;2. Read the news while waiting&quot;);</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>The order of output is 1, 2, 3, not 1, 3, 2. <code>setTimeout</code> schedules the function and immediately moves on. That surprises everyone at first.</p>\n<p>Even <code>setTimeout(fn, 0)</code> runs after the rest of the current code. JavaScript finishes what it&#39;s doing before it handles anything that was scheduled.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"quiz","title":"Quick check","question":"<p>In what order are the letters printed?</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"static\"><pre><code>console.log(&quot;A&quot;);\nsetTimeout(() =&gt; console.log(&quot;B&quot;), 0);\nconsole.log(&quot;C&quot;);</code></pre></div>\n","options":["A B C","A C B","B A C","C A B"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Even with a 0 ms delay, the timer callback waits until the current code has finished. So A, then C, then B.</p>\n"},{"type":"step","title":"Promises","html":"<p>A <strong>Promise</strong> is an object that stands for a value you&#39;ll have <strong>later</strong>. It either <strong>resolves</strong> (succeeds with a value) or <strong>rejects</strong> (fails with an error).</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>function wait(ms) {\n  return new Promise((resolve) =&gt; {\n    setTimeout(resolve, ms);\n  });\n}\n\nfunction rollDiceSlowly() {\n  return new Promise((resolve, reject) =&gt; {\n    setTimeout(() =&gt; {\n      const roll = Math.floor(Math.random() * 6) + 1;\n      if (roll === 1) {\n        reject(new Error(&quot;Rolled a 1. You lose!&quot;));\n      } else {\n        resolve(roll);\n      }\n    }, 500);\n  });\n}\n\nrollDiceSlowly()\n  .then((roll) =&gt; console.log(&quot;You rolled&quot;, roll))\n  .catch((error) =&gt; console.log(&quot;Oops:&quot;, error.message));\n\nconsole.log(&quot;Rolling...&quot;);</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>Run it a few times. Sometimes it fails.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>.then(fn)</code> runs <code>fn</code> with the value once the promise resolves.</li>\n<li><code>.catch(fn)</code> runs <code>fn</code> with the error if it rejects.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>You&#39;ll rarely write <code>new Promise</code> yourself. Most of the time a library or the browser gives you promises (for example <code>fetch(url)</code> returns one), and you just need to use them. And the nicest way to use them is <code>async</code>/<code>await</code>.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"async and await","html":"<p><code>await</code> pauses an <strong><code>async</code> function</strong> until a promise is done, then gives you its value. Async code then reads top to bottom like normal code.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>const wait = (ms) =&gt; new Promise((resolve) =&gt; setTimeout(resolve, ms));\n\nasync function makeBreakfast() {\n  console.log(&quot;Boiling water...&quot;);\n  await wait(500);\n  console.log(&quot;Making tea...&quot;);\n  await wait(500);\n  console.log(&quot;Breakfast is ready&quot;);\n  return &quot;tea&quot;;\n}\n\nmakeBreakfast().then((drink) =&gt; console.log(&quot;Enjoy your&quot;, drink));</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>Things to remember:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>await</code> only works inside a function marked <code>async</code> (or at the top level of a module, which this site allows).</li>\n<li>An <code>async</code> function <strong>always returns a promise</strong>, even if you <code>return &quot;tea&quot;</code>. Whoever calls it needs to <code>await</code> it or use <code>.then</code>.</li>\n<li>Errors in <code>async</code> code are caught with ordinary <code>try</code>/<code>catch</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"A fake web API","html":"<p>Real apps load data from servers. We can&#39;t call a real server here, so this <code>fetchUser</code> function <strong>pretends</strong>: it waits a little, then returns a user or fails.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>const users = { 1: { id: 1, name: &quot;Ada&quot; }, 2: { id: 2, name: &quot;Linus&quot; } };\n\nfunction fetchUser(id) {\n  return new Promise((resolve, reject) =&gt; {\n    setTimeout(() =&gt; {\n      if (users[id]) resolve(users[id]);\n      else reject(new Error(`User ${id} not found`));\n    }, 300);\n  });\n}\n\nasync function showUser(id) {\n  try {\n    const user = await fetchUser(id);\n    console.log(`Found ${user.name}`);\n  } catch (error) {\n    console.log(`Problem: ${error.message}`);\n  }\n}\n\nawait showUser(1);\nawait showUser(99);</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>With a real API you&#39;d write something like:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"static\"><pre><code>const response = await fetch(&quot;https://api.example.com/users/1&quot;);\nconst user = await response.json();</code></pre></div>\n<p>The structure is exactly the same: <code>await</code> the slow thing, and wrap it in <code>try</code>/<code>catch</code>.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"Doing several things at once","html":"<p>Awaiting one thing after another is slow when the tasks don&#39;t depend on each other. <code>Promise.all</code> starts them all together and waits for every one.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>const wait = (ms, value) =&gt; new Promise((resolve) =&gt; setTimeout(() =&gt; resolve(value), ms));\n\nconst start = Date.now();\n\nconst [a, b, c] = await Promise.all([\n  wait(500, &quot;first&quot;),\n  wait(500, &quot;second&quot;),\n  wait(500, &quot;third&quot;),\n]);\n\nconsole.log(a, b, c);\nconsole.log(`Took about ${Math.round((Date.now() - start) / 100) * 100} ms, not 1500`);</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>If any of the promises rejects, <code>Promise.all</code> rejects too. If you want every result whether it failed or not, there&#39;s <code>Promise.allSettled</code>.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"quiz","title":"Quick check","question":"<p>What does an <code>async</code> function always return?</p>\n","options":["Whatever value you <code>return</code>","A Promise","<code>undefined</code>","A callback"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Even <code>async function f() { return 5; }</code> returns a Promise that resolves to 5. You need <code>await f()</code> to get the 5.</p>\n"},{"type":"step","title":"Week 2 done","html":"<p>You now know the core of JavaScript: values, variables, decisions, loops, functions, arrays, objects, errors and async code. That&#39;s the language TypeScript is built on.</p>\n<p>Next week you add <strong>types</strong>. You&#39;ll find that TypeScript mostly asks you to write down things you already know, like &quot;this parameter is a number&quot;, and in return it catches whole categories of bugs before your code ever runs.</p>\n"},{"type":"exercise","id":"js-6-ex1","title":"Safe division","lang":"js","starter":"function divide(a, b) {\n  // Your code here\n\n}\n\nconsole.log(divide(10, 2));\n\ntry {\n  divide(1, 0);\n} catch (error) {\n  console.log(\"Caught:\", error.message);\n}","tests":"test(\"divide(10, 2) is 5\", () => expect(divide(10, 2)).toBe(5));\ntest(\"throws when dividing by zero\", () => expect(() => divide(1, 0)).toThrow(\"Cannot divide by zero\"));","solution":"function divide(a, b) {\n  if (b === 0) {\n    throw new Error(\"Cannot divide by zero\");\n  }\n  return a / b;\n}\n\nconsole.log(divide(10, 2));\n\ntry {\n  divide(1, 0);\n} catch (error) {\n  console.log(\"Caught:\", error.message);\n}","hints":["<p><code>if (b === 0) { throw new Error(&quot;Cannot divide by zero&quot;); }</code></p>\n"],"html":"<p>Write <code>divide(a, b)</code> that returns <code>a / b</code>, but <strong>throws</strong> an <code>Error</code> with the message <code>&quot;Cannot divide by zero&quot;</code> when <code>b</code> is <code>0</code>.</p>\n"},{"type":"exercise","id":"js-6-ex2","title":"Parse a number safely","lang":"js","starter":"function toNumber(text) {\n  // Your code here\n\n}\n\nconsole.log(toNumber(\"42\"));\nconsole.log(toNumber(\"hello\"));","tests":"test('toNumber(\"42\") is 42', () => expect(toNumber(\"42\")).toBe(42));\ntest('toNumber(\"3.5\") is 3.5', () => expect(toNumber(\"3.5\")).toBe(3.5));\ntest('toNumber(\"hello\") is 0', () => expect(toNumber(\"hello\")).toBe(0));","solution":"function toNumber(text) {\n  const n = Number(text);\n  if (Number.isNaN(n)) {\n    return 0;\n  }\n  return n;\n}\n\nconsole.log(toNumber(\"42\"));\nconsole.log(toNumber(\"hello\"));","hints":["<p><code>const n = Number(text);</code> then check <code>Number.isNaN(n)</code>.</p>\n"],"html":"<p>Write <code>toNumber(text)</code> that converts a string to a number. If the result is <code>NaN</code>, return <code>0</code> instead. Use <code>Number(text)</code> and <code>Number.isNaN(...)</code>.</p>\n"},{"type":"exercise","id":"js-6-ex3","title":"Get a user's name","lang":"js","starter":"const users = { 1: { id: 1, name: \"Ada\" }, 2: { id: 2, name: \"Linus\" } };\nfunction fetchUser(id) {\n  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\n    setTimeout(() => {\n      if (users[id]) resolve(users[id]);\n      else reject(new Error(`User ${id} not found`));\n    }, 100);\n  });\n}\n\n// Write getUserName here\n\n\nconsole.log(await getUserName(1));\nconsole.log(await getUserName(42));","tests":"test(\"getUserName(1) resolves to 'Ada'\", async () => expect(await getUserName(1)).toBe(\"Ada\"));\ntest(\"getUserName(2) resolves to 'Linus'\", async () => expect(await getUserName(2)).toBe(\"Linus\"));\ntest(\"an unknown id gives 'Unknown'\", async () => expect(await getUserName(42)).toBe(\"Unknown\"));\ntest(\"it returns a Promise\", () => expect(getUserName(1) instanceof Promise).toBe(true));","solution":"const users = { 1: { id: 1, name: \"Ada\" }, 2: { id: 2, name: \"Linus\" } };\nfunction fetchUser(id) {\n  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\n    setTimeout(() => {\n      if (users[id]) resolve(users[id]);\n      else reject(new Error(`User ${id} not found`));\n    }, 100);\n  });\n}\n\nasync function getUserName(id) {\n  try {\n    const user = await fetchUser(id);\n    return user.name;\n  } catch (error) {\n    return \"Unknown\";\n  }\n}\n\nconsole.log(await getUserName(1));\nconsole.log(await getUserName(42));","hints":["<p>Start with <code>async function getUserName(id) { ... }</code>.</p>\n","<p>Inside, <code>await fetchUser(id)</code> in a <code>try</code> block, and <code>return &quot;Unknown&quot;</code> in the <code>catch</code> block.</p>\n"],"html":"<p><code>fetchUser(id)</code> is given. It returns a Promise for a user object, or rejects if the user doesn&#39;t exist.</p>\n<p>Write an <strong>async</strong> function <code>getUserName(id)</code> that returns the user&#39;s <code>name</code>, or the string <code>&quot;Unknown&quot;</code> if fetching fails.</p>\n"},{"type":"exercise","id":"js-6-ex4","title":"Load them all","lang":"js","starter":"const users = { 1: { id: 1, name: \"Ada\" }, 2: { id: 2, name: \"Linus\" }, 3: { id: 3, name: \"Grace\" } };\nfunction fetchUser(id) {\n  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\n    setTimeout(() => {\n      if (users[id]) resolve(users[id]);\n      else reject(new Error(`User ${id} not found`));\n    }, 300);\n  });\n}\n\n// Write getNames here\n\n\nconsole.log(await getNames([3, 1, 2]));","tests":"test(\"returns names in order\", async () => expect(await getNames([3, 1, 2])).toEqual([\"Grace\", \"Ada\", \"Linus\"]));\ntest(\"runs the requests at the same time\", async () => {\n  const start = Date.now();\n  await getNames([1, 2, 3]);\n  expect(Date.now() - start < 600).toBe(true);\n});","solution":"const users = { 1: { id: 1, name: \"Ada\" }, 2: { id: 2, name: \"Linus\" }, 3: { id: 3, name: \"Grace\" } };\nfunction fetchUser(id) {\n  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\n    setTimeout(() => {\n      if (users[id]) resolve(users[id]);\n      else reject(new Error(`User ${id} not found`));\n    }, 300);\n  });\n}\n\nasync function getNames(ids) {\n  const found = await Promise.all(ids.map((id) => fetchUser(id)));\n  return found.map((user) => user.name);\n}\n\nconsole.log(await getNames([3, 1, 2]));","hints":["<p><code>ids.map((id) =&gt; fetchUser(id))</code> gives you an array of promises.</p>\n","<p><code>await Promise.all(thatArray)</code> gives you an array of users. Then <code>map</code> them to names.</p>\n"],"html":"<p>Using the same <code>fetchUser</code>, write an async function <code>getNames(ids)</code> that fetches all the users <strong>at the same time</strong> with <code>Promise.all</code> and returns an array of their names, in the same order as <code>ids</code>.</p>\n"}]},{"id":"ts-1","week":3,"session":1,"lang":"ts","title":"Hello, TypeScript","minutes":60,"summary":"See what TypeScript adds to JavaScript, write your first type annotations, and read type errors.","goals":["Explain what TypeScript does and when it does it","Annotate variables, parameters and return values","Let type inference do most of the work","Type arrays and tuples","Choose between <code>any</code> and <code>unknown</code>"],"sections":[{"type":"step","title":"The bug TypeScript catches","html":"<p>Here is some JavaScript with a bug. Press <strong>Run</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>function totalPrice(price, quantity) {\n  return price * quantity;\n}\n\nconst fromForm = &quot;3&quot;;           // values from forms are always strings\nconsole.log(totalPrice(9.99, fromForm));\nconsole.log(totalPrice(9.99));  // forgot an argument</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>The first call happens to work, by luck, because <code>*</code> converts the string. The second gives <code>NaN</code>. Neither one warns you. In a real app, this kind of bug can sit unnoticed until a customer sees &quot;NaN&quot; on their invoice.</p>\n<p>Now the same code in TypeScript, with types on the parameters. Run it:</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"only-slide\"><p>Now the same code in TypeScript. Run it:</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>function totalPrice(price: number, quantity: number): number {\n  return price * quantity;\n}\n\nconst fromForm = &quot;3&quot;;\nconsole.log(totalPrice(9.99, fromForm));\nconsole.log(totalPrice(9.99));</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>TypeScript reports both problems, with the line number, <strong>before</strong> the code runs. In a code editor like VS Code, these show up as red squiggles while you type.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"What TypeScript actually is","html":"<p>TypeScript is JavaScript plus <strong>type annotations</strong>. Running it is a two-step process:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The TypeScript <strong>compiler</strong> checks your types and reports errors.</li>\n<li>It then <strong>removes the types</strong>, leaving plain JavaScript, which is what actually runs.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Types never exist while the program runs. They are a safety check while you write it.</p>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>That&#39;s why this site shows type errors and <em>then</em> still runs the code: by default the compiler still produces JavaScript even when it finds errors. In a real project you&#39;d fix every error before shipping.</p>\n<p>Every JavaScript program is already almost valid TypeScript. Everything you learned in weeks 1 and 2 still applies. You&#39;re just adding information.</p>\n<p>In a real project you install TypeScript with <code>npm install typescript</code> and run the compiler with <code>npx tsc</code>. Tools like Vite, Next.js and Node.js (version 22.6 and later) can also run TypeScript files directly. On this site, it all happens in your browser.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"Your first annotations","html":"<p>Add a type after a variable name with a colon. The basic types are the ones you already know: <code>string</code>, <code>number</code>, <code>boolean</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>let username: string = &quot;ada&quot;;\nlet age: number = 36;\nlet isAdmin: boolean = false;\n\nage = 37;          // fine\nage = &quot;old&quot;;       // error: a string is not a number\n\nconsole.log(username, age, isAdmin);</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>Type names are lowercase: <code>string</code>, not <code>String</code>. The capitalised versions exist, but they mean something different and you should avoid them.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"Type inference","html":"<p>You don&#39;t need to annotate everything. When you give a variable a value straight away, TypeScript <strong>infers</strong> its type from that value.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>let city = &quot;Oslo&quot;;      // inferred as string\nlet year = 2026;        // inferred as number\n\ncity = 42;              // still an error, even without an annotation\n\nconst pi = 3.14;        // const: inferred as exactly 3.14\nconsole.log(city, year, pi);</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>In practice:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Don&#39;t</strong> annotate variables that get a value immediately. Inference does it, and extra annotations are noise.</li>\n<li><strong>Do</strong> annotate function parameters. TypeScript can&#39;t guess what callers will pass.</li>\n<li>Annotating return types is optional, but it&#39;s a good habit for important functions. It documents intent and catches mistakes inside the function.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If you hover over a variable in VS Code, it shows you the inferred type.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"quiz","title":"Quick check","question":"<p><code>let count = 10;</code> What type does TypeScript give <code>count</code>?</p>\n","options":["<code>any</code>","<code>number</code>","<code>10</code>","No type until you write one"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>TypeScript infers <code>number</code> from the starting value. With <code>const count = 10</code> it would infer the exact value <code>10</code>, because a const can never change.</p>\n"},{"type":"step","title":"Typing functions","html":"<p>Annotate each parameter, and optionally the return type after the brackets.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>function greet(name: string, excited: boolean = false): string {\n  return excited ? `Hello, ${name}!` : `Hello, ${name}.`;\n}\n\nconst add = (a: number, b: number): number =&gt; a + b;\n\nfunction logMessage(message: string): void {\n  console.log(message);\n}\n\nconsole.log(greet(&quot;Ada&quot;));\nconsole.log(add(2, 3));\nlogMessage(greet(&quot;Linus&quot;, true));\nconsole.log(greet(42));</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><ul>\n<li><code>void</code> means &quot;returns nothing useful&quot;. Use it for functions that just do something, like logging.</li>\n<li>Optional parameters get a <code>?</code>: <code>function greet(name: string, title?: string)</code>. Inside the function, <code>title</code> is <code>string | undefined</code>. More on that <code>|</code> in two sessions.</li>\n<li>A parameter with a default value (<code>excited = false</code>) is automatically optional, and its type is inferred from the default.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"Reading type errors","html":"<p>Type errors can look scary but follow a pattern: <strong>&quot;Type X is not assignable to type Y&quot;</strong> means &quot;you gave me an X, but I needed a Y&quot;.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>function square(n: number): number {\n  return n * n;\n}\n\nconst result: string = square(4);\nsquare(&quot;4&quot;);\nconst total = square(2, 3);</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>Read the three errors:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code>square(4)</code> returns a <code>number</code>, but <code>result</code> was declared as a <code>string</code>.</li>\n<li><code>&quot;4&quot;</code> is a string, but the parameter needs a number.</li>\n<li><code>square</code> takes 1 argument, but got 2.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Always read the <strong>first</strong> error first. Later errors are often side effects of earlier ones.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"Arrays and tuples","html":"<p>Write an array type as <code>type[]</code>. Every item must match.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>const scores: number[] = [90, 72, 85];\nconst names: string[] = [&quot;Ada&quot;, &quot;Linus&quot;];\nconst inferred = [true, false];      // boolean[]\n\nscores.push(100);       // fine\nscores.push(&quot;100&quot;);     // error\n\nconst doubled = scores.map((s) =&gt; s * 2);   // TypeScript knows this is number[]\nconsole.log(doubled);\n\n// A tuple: fixed length, a type for each position\nconst point: [number, number] = [3, 4];\nconst entry: [string, number] = [&quot;age&quot;, 36];\nconsole.log(point[0] + point[1], entry[0]);</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>You may also see <code>Array&lt;number&gt;</code>, which means the same as <code>number[]</code>.</p>\n<p>Notice that in <code>scores.map((s) =&gt; s * 2)</code> you didn&#39;t write a type for <code>s</code>. TypeScript already knows <code>scores</code> holds numbers, so it knows <code>s</code> is a number. This is called <strong>contextual typing</strong>, and it&#39;s why TypeScript code is usually much less cluttered than you&#39;d expect.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"any and unknown","html":"<p>Sometimes you don&#39;t know a value&#39;s type, for example data parsed from JSON.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>any</code> switches type checking <strong>off</strong>. Anything goes, and you lose all protection.</li>\n<li><code>unknown</code> means &quot;could be anything, so <strong>check before you use it</strong>&quot;. It&#39;s the safe choice.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>const a: any = JSON.parse('&quot;hello&quot;');\na.toFixed(2);          // no error from TypeScript... but it crashes when run\n\nconst u: unknown = JSON.parse('&quot;hello&quot;');\nu.toUpperCase();       // error: you must check what it is first\n\nif (typeof u === &quot;string&quot;) {\n  console.log(u.toUpperCase());   // fine: inside this if, u is a string\n}</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>Avoid <code>any</code>. Each <code>any</code> is a hole in your safety net, and they spread: anything you get from an <code>any</code> value is also <code>any</code>.</p>\n<p>That <code>typeof</code> check is called <strong>narrowing</strong>. TypeScript understands it and treats <code>u</code> as a string inside the <code>if</code>. Narrowing is a big topic, coming in session 3.</p>\n<p>Note that there&#39;s a runtime error in the output here: <code>a.toFixed is not a function</code>. TypeScript couldn&#39;t warn you, because you told it to stop checking.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"quiz","title":"Quick check","question":"<p>Which is the safer type for a value you know nothing about?</p>\n","options":["<code>any</code>","<code>unknown</code>","<code>object</code>","<code>never</code>"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p><code>unknown</code> forces you to check the value before you use it. <code>any</code> turns type checking off.</p>\n"},{"type":"exercise","id":"ts-1-ex1","title":"Add the types","lang":"ts","starter":"function fullName(first, last) {\n  return `${first} ${last}`;\n}\n\nconsole.log(fullName(\"Ada\", \"Lovelace\"));","tests":"test('fullName(\"Ada\", \"Lovelace\") is \"Ada Lovelace\"', () => expect(fullName(\"Ada\", \"Lovelace\")).toBe(\"Ada Lovelace\"));","solution":"function fullName(first: string, last: string): string {\n  return `${first} ${last}`;\n}\n\nconsole.log(fullName(\"Ada\", \"Lovelace\"));","hints":["<p><code>function fullName(first: string, last: string): string { ... }</code></p>\n"],"html":"<p>This function works, but its parameters have no types, so TypeScript complains that they are implicitly <code>any</code>. Add types to the parameters <strong>and</strong> a return type.</p>\n"},{"type":"exercise","id":"ts-1-ex2","title":"Fix the type errors","lang":"ts","starter":"let count: number = \"3\";\nconst names: string[] = [\"Ada\", \"Linus\", 7];\n\nfunction isLong(text: string): boolean {\n  return text.length;\n}\n\nconst longName = isLong(names[0]);\nconsole.log(count, names, longName);","tests":"test(\"count is the number 3\", () => expect(count).toBe(3));\ntest(\"names has three strings\", () => expect(names.every((n) => typeof n === \"string\") && names.length === 3).toBe(true));\ntest('isLong(\"Ada\") is false (3 characters is not long)', () => expect(isLong(\"Ada\")).toBe(false));\ntest('isLong(\"Margaret\") is true', () => expect(isLong(\"Margaret\")).toBe(true));","solution":"let count: number = 3;\nconst names: string[] = [\"Ada\", \"Linus\", \"Grace\"];\n\nfunction isLong(text: string): boolean {\n  return text.length > 5;\n}\n\nconst longName = isLong(names[0]);\nconsole.log(count, names, longName);","hints":["<p><code>&quot;3&quot;</code> in quotes is a string. A list of <code>string[]</code> can&#39;t contain the number <code>7</code>.</p>\n","<p><code>text.length</code> is a number, but the function promises a boolean. Return a comparison instead, like <code>text.length &gt; 5</code>.</p>\n"],"html":"<p>Run this to see the type errors, then fix them. Change the <strong>values or code</strong>, not the type annotations, so that everything is consistent.</p>\n"},{"type":"exercise","id":"ts-1-ex3","title":"Typed average","lang":"ts","starter":"// Write average here\n\n\nconsole.log(average([2, 4, 9]));","tests":"test(\"average([2, 4, 9]) is 5\", () => expect(average([2, 4, 9])).toBe(5));\ntest(\"average([]) is 0\", () => expect(average([])).toBe(0));","solution":"function average(numbers: number[]): number {\n  if (numbers.length === 0) {\n    return 0;\n  }\n  const total = numbers.reduce((sum, n) => sum + n, 0);\n  return total / numbers.length;\n}\n\nconsole.log(average([2, 4, 9]));","hints":["<p><code>function average(numbers: number[]): number { ... }</code>. The body is the same as the JavaScript version from week 2.</p>\n"],"html":"<p>Write <code>average(numbers: number[]): number</code>. Return <code>0</code> for an empty array.</p>\n"},{"type":"exercise","id":"ts-1-ex4","title":"Parse a list of numbers","lang":"ts","starter":"function parseNumbers(input: string): number[] {\n  // Your code here\n\n}\n\nconsole.log(parseNumbers(\"4, 8,15\"));\nconsole.log(parseNumbers(\"1, two, 3\"));","tests":"test('\"4, 8,15\" gives [4, 8, 15]', () => expect(parseNumbers(\"4, 8,15\")).toEqual([4, 8, 15]));\ntest('\"1, two, 3\" gives [1, 3]', () => expect(parseNumbers(\"1, two, 3\")).toEqual([1, 3]));\ntest('\"42\" gives [42]', () => expect(parseNumbers(\"42\")).toEqual([42]));","solution":"function parseNumbers(input: string): number[] {\n  return input\n    .split(\",\")\n    .map((part) => Number(part.trim()))\n    .filter((n) => !Number.isNaN(n));\n}\n\nconsole.log(parseNumbers(\"4, 8,15\"));\nconsole.log(parseNumbers(\"1, two, 3\"));","hints":["<p><code>input.split(&quot;,&quot;)</code> gives you the parts. <code>part.trim()</code> removes the spaces around each one.</p>\n","<p><code>map</code> each part to <code>Number(...)</code>, then <code>filter</code> out the <code>NaN</code>s with <code>Number.isNaN</code>.</p>\n"],"html":"<p>Write <code>parseNumbers(input: string): number[]</code> that turns text like <code>&quot;4, 8,15&quot;</code> into <code>[4, 8, 15]</code>. Skip any part that isn&#39;t a number.</p>\n"}]},{"id":"ts-2","week":3,"session":2,"lang":"ts","title":"Describing objects","minutes":60,"summary":"Describe the shape of your data with type aliases and interfaces, including optional and read-only properties.","goals":["Write object types inline, with <code>type</code> and with <code>interface</code>","Mark properties optional (<code>?</code>) or <code>readonly</code>","Extend interfaces to build bigger types","Type functions and dictionaries"],"sections":[{"type":"step","title":"Object types","html":"<p>In JavaScript you can put anything into an object. In TypeScript you describe the <strong>shape</strong> an object must have: which properties, and what type each one is.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>const book: { title: string; pages: number } = {\n  title: &quot;Dune&quot;,\n  pages: 412,\n};\n\nconsole.log(book.title.toUpperCase());\nconsole.log(book.author);     // error: no such property\nbook.pages = &quot;many&quot;;          // error: wrong type</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>TypeScript now knows exactly what&#39;s in <code>book</code>. That means your editor can autocomplete property names, and typos like <code>book.titel</code> are caught immediately.</p>\n<p>Writing the shape inline gets long and repetitive, though. Next, we give the shape a name.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"Naming a shape with type","html":"<p>A <strong>type alias</strong> gives a name to a type, so you can reuse it.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>type Book = {\n  title: string;\n  author: string;\n  pages: number;\n};\n\nconst dune: Book = { title: &quot;Dune&quot;, author: &quot;Frank Herbert&quot;, pages: 412 };\nconst emma: Book = { title: &quot;Emma&quot;, author: &quot;Jane Austen&quot; };   // error: pages is missing\n\nfunction describe(book: Book): string {\n  return `${book.title} by ${book.author}`;\n}\n\nconsole.log(describe(dune));</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>By convention, type names start with a capital letter: <code>Book</code>, <code>User</code>, <code>OrderItem</code>.</p>\n<p><code>type</code> can name any type, not just objects: <code>type Id = string;</code> or <code>type Score = number;</code>. You&#39;ll use that a lot next session with unions.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"Interfaces","html":"<p>An <strong>interface</strong> is another way to name an object shape. For objects, it does almost the same job as <code>type</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>interface User {\n  id: number;\n  name: string;\n  email: string;\n}\n\nfunction welcome(user: User): string {\n  return `Welcome back, ${user.name}!`;\n}\n\nconst ada: User = { id: 1, name: &quot;Ada&quot;, email: &quot;ada@example.com&quot; };\nconsole.log(welcome(ada));\nconsole.log(welcome({ id: 2, name: &quot;Linus&quot; }));</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p><strong><code>type</code> or <code>interface</code>?</strong> Both work, and teams argue about it. A simple rule that works well:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Use <code>interface</code> for object shapes, especially ones that other shapes build on.</li>\n<li>Use <code>type</code> for everything else: unions, tuples, function types and aliases.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Whichever you choose, be consistent within a project.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"Optional and readonly properties","html":"<ul>\n<li><code>?</code> after a name makes the property <strong>optional</strong>: it may be missing.</li>\n<li><code>readonly</code> means it can be set when the object is created, but not changed after.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>interface Product {\n  readonly id: number;\n  name: string;\n  price: number;\n  description?: string;\n}\n\nconst lamp: Product = { id: 1, name: &quot;Lamp&quot;, price: 30 };   // no description: fine\n\nlamp.price = 25;     // fine\nlamp.id = 2;         // error: id is readonly\n\nconsole.log(lamp.description);              // undefined\nconsole.log(lamp.description.length);       // error: it might be undefined\nconsole.log(lamp.description?.length);      // safe: ?. stops if it's undefined</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>Because <code>description</code> is optional, its type is really <code>string | undefined</code>. TypeScript won&#39;t let you use it as a string until you&#39;ve handled the <code>undefined</code> case. The <code>?.</code> operator (optional chaining) gives back <code>undefined</code> instead of crashing when the value on its left is missing. More on this next session.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"quiz","title":"Quick check","question":"<p><code>interface Pet { name: string; age?: number }</code>. Which object is <strong>not</strong> a valid <code>Pet</code>?</p>\n","options":["<code>{ name: &quot;Rex&quot; }</code>","<code>{ name: &quot;Rex&quot;, age: 3 }</code>","<code>{ age: 3 }</code>","<code>{ name: &quot;Rex&quot;, age: undefined }</code>"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p><code>name</code> is required. Only <code>age</code> is optional.</p>\n"},{"type":"step","title":"Extending interfaces","html":"<p>An interface can <strong>extend</strong> another, taking all its properties and adding more.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>interface Animal {\n  name: string;\n  legs: number;\n}\n\ninterface Pet extends Animal {\n  owner: string;\n}\n\nconst dog: Pet = { name: &quot;Rex&quot;, legs: 4, owner: &quot;Ada&quot; };\n\nfunction intro(animal: Animal): string {\n  return `${animal.name} has ${animal.legs} legs`;\n}\n\nconsole.log(intro(dog));   // a Pet is also an Animal</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>Notice that <code>intro</code> asks for an <code>Animal</code>, and we passed a <code>Pet</code>. That&#39;s allowed because a <code>Pet</code> has everything an <code>Animal</code> needs. TypeScript checks <strong>shapes</strong>, not names. This is called <strong>structural typing</strong>: if it has the right properties, it fits.</p>\n<p>With <code>type</code>, you do the same thing using <code>&amp;</code> (an <strong>intersection</strong>):</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>type Animal = { name: string; legs: number };\ntype Pet = Animal &amp; { owner: string };\n\nconst cat: Pet = { name: &quot;Tom&quot;, legs: 4, owner: &quot;Grace&quot; };\nconsole.log(cat);</code></pre></div>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"Excess properties","html":"<p>When you write an object literal directly where a type is expected, TypeScript also checks you didn&#39;t add <strong>extra</strong> properties. Usually that means you made a typo.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>interface Settings {\n  theme: string;\n  fontSize: number;\n}\n\nconst s: Settings = {\n  theme: &quot;dark&quot;,\n  fontsize: 14,     // typo! error: did you mean fontSize?\n};\nconsole.log(s);</code></pre></div>\n"},{"type":"step","title":"Typing functions","html":"<p>A function&#39;s type describes its parameters and return value, written with an arrow.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>type MathOp = (a: number, b: number) =&gt; number;\n\nconst add: MathOp = (a, b) =&gt; a + b;        // a and b are inferred as number\nconst multiply: MathOp = (a, b) =&gt; a * b;\n\nfunction calculate(op: MathOp, x: number, y: number): number {\n  return op(x, y);\n}\n\nconsole.log(calculate(add, 3, 4));\nconsole.log(calculate(multiply, 3, 4));\nconsole.log(calculate((a, b) =&gt; a - b, 10, 4));</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>Objects can include methods in their types too:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>interface Counter {\n  count: number;\n  increment(): void;\n  add(amount: number): number;\n}\n\nconst c: Counter = {\n  count: 0,\n  increment() { this.count++; },\n  add(amount) { this.count += amount; return this.count; },\n};\n\nc.increment();\nconsole.log(c.add(5));</code></pre></div>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"Dictionaries with Record","html":"<p>When an object is used as a lookup table with keys you don&#39;t know in advance, describe it with <code>Record&lt;KeyType, ValueType&gt;</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>const stock: Record&lt;string, number&gt; = {\n  apples: 5,\n  pears: 0,\n};\n\nstock.plums = 12;          // any string key is fine\nstock.kiwis = &quot;lots&quot;;      // error: values must be numbers\n\nfor (const [fruit, count] of Object.entries(stock)) {\n  console.log(fruit, count);\n}\n\ntype Day = &quot;mon&quot; | &quot;tue&quot; | &quot;wed&quot;;\nconst hours: Record&lt;Day, number&gt; = { mon: 8, tue: 6, wed: 7 };\nconsole.log(hours.tue);</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>The second example uses a <strong>union of strings</strong> as the key type, so the object must have exactly those three keys. You&#39;ll learn about unions next session.</p>\n<p>You may also see the older syntax <code>{ [key: string]: number }</code>, called an index signature. It means the same as <code>Record&lt;string, number&gt;</code>.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"quiz","title":"Quick check","question":"<p>Why is it fine to pass a <code>Pet</code> (with <code>name</code>, <code>legs</code> and <code>owner</code>) to a function that wants an <code>Animal</code> (with <code>name</code> and <code>legs</code>)?</p>\n","options":["Because <code>Pet</code> and <code>Animal</code> have similar names","Because a <code>Pet</code> has every property an <code>Animal</code> needs","Because TypeScript ignores extra properties in all cases","It isn&#39;t fine; it&#39;s an error"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>TypeScript compares shapes. Any value with at least the required properties fits. (Extra properties are only flagged when you write an object literal directly.)</p>\n"},{"type":"exercise","id":"ts-2-ex1","title":"Products","lang":"ts","starter":"// Define the Product interface here\n\n\nfunction formatProduct(p: Product): string {\n  // Your code here\n\n}\n\nconst lamp: Product = { name: \"Lamp\", price: 19.99, inStock: true };\nconst desk: Product = { name: \"Desk\", price: 150, inStock: false };\nconsole.log(formatProduct(lamp));\nconsole.log(formatProduct(desk));","tests":"test(\"formats an in-stock product\", () =>\n  expect(formatProduct({ name: \"Lamp\", price: 19.99, inStock: true })).toBe(\"Lamp: $19.99 (in stock)\"));\ntest(\"formats a sold-out product\", () =>\n  expect(formatProduct({ name: \"Desk\", price: 150, inStock: false })).toBe(\"Desk: $150.00 (sold out)\"));","solution":"interface Product {\n  name: string;\n  price: number;\n  inStock: boolean;\n}\n\nfunction formatProduct(p: Product): string {\n  const status = p.inStock ? \"in stock\" : \"sold out\";\n  return `${p.name}: $${p.price.toFixed(2)} (${status})`;\n}\n\nconst lamp: Product = { name: \"Lamp\", price: 19.99, inStock: true };\nconst desk: Product = { name: \"Desk\", price: 150, inStock: false };\nconsole.log(formatProduct(lamp));\nconsole.log(formatProduct(desk));","hints":["<p><code>interface Product { name: string; price: number; inStock: boolean; }</code></p>\n","<p>A ternary picks the status: <code>p.inStock ? &quot;in stock&quot; : &quot;sold out&quot;</code>. In a template literal, a dollar sign followed by <code>${...}</code> is written <code>$${...}</code>.</p>\n"],"html":"<p>Create an <code>interface Product</code> with <code>name</code> (string), <code>price</code> (number) and <code>inStock</code> (boolean) so the code below has no type errors. Then finish <code>formatProduct</code> so it returns text like:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>Lamp: $19.99 (in stock)</code></li>\n<li><code>Desk: $150.00 (sold out)</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Use <code>price.toFixed(2)</code> to show two decimals.</p>\n"},{"type":"exercise","id":"ts-2-ex2","title":"Optional email","lang":"ts","starter":"interface Contact {\n  name: string;\n  email?: string;\n}\n\nfunction contactLine(contact: Contact): string {\n  // Your code here\n\n}\n\nconsole.log(contactLine({ name: \"Ada\", email: \"ada@example.com\" }));\nconsole.log(contactLine({ name: \"Linus\" }));","tests":"test(\"shows the email when present\", () =>\n  expect(contactLine({ name: \"Ada\", email: \"ada@example.com\" })).toBe(\"Ada <ada@example.com>\"));\ntest(\"says (no email) when missing\", () => expect(contactLine({ name: \"Linus\" })).toBe(\"Linus (no email)\"));","solution":"interface Contact {\n  name: string;\n  email?: string;\n}\n\nfunction contactLine(contact: Contact): string {\n  if (contact.email) {\n    return `${contact.name} <${contact.email}>`;\n  }\n  return `${contact.name} (no email)`;\n}\n\nconsole.log(contactLine({ name: \"Ada\", email: \"ada@example.com\" }));\nconsole.log(contactLine({ name: \"Linus\" }));","hints":["<p>Check <code>if (contact.email) { ... }</code> first.</p>\n"],"html":"<p>The <code>Contact</code> interface below has an <strong>optional</strong> <code>email</code>. Finish <code>contactLine</code> so it returns:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>Ada &lt;ada@example.com&gt;</code> when there is an email</li>\n<li><code>Linus (no email)</code> when there isn&#39;t</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"type":"exercise","id":"ts-2-ex3","title":"Extend an interface","lang":"ts","starter":"interface Person {\n  name: string;\n  age: number;\n}\n\n// interface Employee ...\n\n\n// function raise ...\n\n\nconst sam: Employee = { name: \"Sam\", age: 30, role: \"Developer\", salary: 50000 };\nconsole.log(raise(sam, 10));","tests":"const sam2 = { name: \"Sam\", age: 30, role: \"Developer\", salary: 50000 };\ntest(\"raises the salary by 10%\", () => expect(raise(sam2, 10).salary).toBeCloseTo(55000));\ntest(\"keeps the other properties\", () => {\n  const r = raise(sam2, 10);\n  expect(r.name).toBe(\"Sam\");\n  expect(r.role).toBe(\"Developer\");\n});\ntest(\"doesn't change the original\", () => {\n  raise(sam2, 50);\n  expect(sam2.salary).toBe(50000);\n});","solution":"interface Person {\n  name: string;\n  age: number;\n}\n\ninterface Employee extends Person {\n  role: string;\n  salary: number;\n}\n\nfunction raise(employee: Employee, percent: number): Employee {\n  return { ...employee, salary: employee.salary * (1 + percent / 100) };\n}\n\nconst sam: Employee = { name: \"Sam\", age: 30, role: \"Developer\", salary: 50000 };\nconsole.log(raise(sam, 10));","hints":["<p><code>interface Employee extends Person { role: string; salary: number; }</code></p>\n","<p>Return <code>{ ...employee, salary: ... }</code>. A 10% raise multiplies the salary by <code>1 + 10 / 100</code>.</p>\n"],"html":"<p>Create an interface <code>Employee</code> that <strong>extends</strong> <code>Person</code> and adds <code>role</code> (string) and <code>salary</code> (number). Then write <code>raise(employee: Employee, percent: number): Employee</code> that returns a <strong>new</strong> employee with the salary increased by that percent.</p>\n"},{"type":"exercise","id":"ts-2-ex4","title":"Function types","lang":"ts","starter":"type Formatter = (value: number) => string;\n\n// function formatAll ...\n\n\n// const asPercent: Formatter = ...\n\n\nconsole.log(formatAll([0.25, 0.5, 1], asPercent));","tests":"test(\"asPercent(0.25) is '25%'\", () => expect(asPercent(0.25)).toBe(\"25%\"));\ntest(\"formatAll applies the formatter\", () => expect(formatAll([0.25, 0.5, 1], asPercent)).toEqual([\"25%\", \"50%\", \"100%\"]));\ntest(\"formatAll works with any formatter\", () => expect(formatAll([1, 2], (n) => `#${n}`)).toEqual([\"#1\", \"#2\"]));","solution":"type Formatter = (value: number) => string;\n\nfunction formatAll(values: number[], format: Formatter): string[] {\n  return values.map(format);\n}\n\nconst asPercent: Formatter = (value) => `${Math.round(value * 100)}%`;\n\nconsole.log(formatAll([0.25, 0.5, 1], asPercent));","hints":["<p><code>formatAll</code> is a one-liner with <code>map</code>.</p>\n","<p><code>const asPercent: Formatter = (value) =&gt; ...</code>. You don&#39;t need to annotate <code>value</code>; the <code>Formatter</code> type already says it&#39;s a number.</p>\n"],"html":"<p>A type <code>Formatter</code> is given. Write <code>formatAll(values: number[], format: Formatter): string[]</code> that applies the formatter to every value. Then create a <code>Formatter</code> called <code>asPercent</code> that turns <code>0.25</code> into <code>&quot;25%&quot;</code>.</p>\n"}]},{"id":"ts-3","week":3,"session":3,"lang":"ts","title":"Unions and narrowing","minutes":60,"summary":"Say &quot;this or that&quot; with union types, then prove to TypeScript which one you have.","goals":["Write union and literal types","Narrow with <code>typeof</code>, <code>in</code>, <code>instanceof</code> and equality checks","Handle <code>null</code> and <code>undefined</code> with <code>?.</code> and <code>??</code>","Model data with discriminated unions","Make the compiler check you handled every case"],"sections":[{"type":"step","title":"Union types","html":"<p>A <strong>union</strong> says a value can be one of several types. Write it with <code>|</code>, read as &quot;or&quot;.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>function formatId(id: string | number): string {\n  return `ID-${id}`;\n}\n\nconsole.log(formatId(42));\nconsole.log(formatId(&quot;abc&quot;));\nconsole.log(formatId(true));   // error: boolean isn't string or number</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>Unions are everywhere in real code: an ID that can be a number or a string, a value that can be a result or <code>null</code>, a status that is one of a few options.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"Narrowing with typeof","html":"<p>With a union, TypeScript only lets you do things that work for <strong>every</strong> member. To do something specific, first <strong>narrow</strong> the type with a check. Inside the check, TypeScript knows which one you have.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>function double(value: string | number) {\n  // value.toUpperCase();   // error: numbers don't have toUpperCase\n\n  if (typeof value === &quot;string&quot;) {\n    return value.repeat(2);   // here value is a string\n  }\n  return value * 2;           // here it must be a number\n}\n\nconsole.log(double(&quot;ha&quot;));\nconsole.log(double(21));</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>TypeScript follows the flow of your code. After the <code>if</code> returns, it knows that only <code>number</code> is left. This is called <strong>control flow analysis</strong>, and it&#39;s what makes TypeScript feel smart rather than strict.</p>\n<p>Other checks TypeScript understands:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>Array.isArray(x)</code> for arrays</li>\n<li><code>x instanceof Date</code> for class instances</li>\n<li><code>&quot;key&quot; in x</code> for objects with a particular property</li>\n<li><code>x === null</code>, <code>x === &quot;admin&quot;</code> and other equality checks</li>\n<li>simple truthiness: <code>if (x) { ... }</code></li>\n</ul>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"Literal types","html":"<p>A type can be an <strong>exact value</strong>. Combine literal types in a union to make a list of allowed options.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>type Direction = &quot;up&quot; | &quot;down&quot; | &quot;left&quot; | &quot;right&quot;;\ntype Size = &quot;S&quot; | &quot;M&quot; | &quot;L&quot;;\ntype DiceRoll = 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6;\n\nfunction move(direction: Direction) {\n  console.log(`Moving ${direction}`);\n}\n\nmove(&quot;up&quot;);\nmove(&quot;north&quot;);    // error: not one of the allowed values\n\nlet shirt: Size = &quot;M&quot;;\nshirt = &quot;XXL&quot;;    // error</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>This replaces a whole category of bugs, like misspelled status strings (&quot;cancelled&quot; vs &quot;canceled&quot;), with compile-time errors. Your editor will also autocomplete the allowed values.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"quiz","title":"Quick check","question":"<p>Given <code>type Status = &quot;open&quot; | &quot;closed&quot;;</code>, which assignment is an error?</p>\n","options":["<code>const s: Status = &quot;open&quot;;</code>","<code>const s: Status = &quot;closed&quot;;</code>","<code>const s: Status = &quot;Open&quot;;</code>","<code>let s: Status = &quot;closed&quot;; s = &quot;open&quot;;</code>"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>Literal types are exact, and case-sensitive. <code>&quot;Open&quot;</code> is not <code>&quot;open&quot;</code>.</p>\n"},{"type":"step","title":"null and undefined","html":"<p>With TypeScript&#39;s <code>strict</code> mode (always on in this course, and on in all new projects), <code>null</code> and <code>undefined</code> aren&#39;t allowed unless the type says so. That means you must deal with &quot;missing&quot; values explicitly.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>function findUser(id: number): string | undefined {\n  const users: Record&lt;number, string&gt; = { 1: &quot;Ada&quot;, 2: &quot;Linus&quot; };\n  return users[id];\n}\n\nconst user = findUser(3);\nconsole.log(user.toUpperCase());     // error: user might be undefined\n\nif (user !== undefined) {\n  console.log(user.toUpperCase());   // fine: narrowed to string\n} else {\n  console.log(&quot;No such user&quot;);\n}</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>This single feature prevents the most common runtime error in JavaScript: &quot;Cannot read properties of undefined&quot;.</p>\n<p><code>Array.prototype.find</code> returns <code>T | undefined</code> for the same reason. The item might not be there.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"Optional chaining and nullish coalescing","html":"<p>Two operators make missing values easy to handle:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>a?.b</code> gives <code>undefined</code> instead of crashing if <code>a</code> is <code>null</code> or <code>undefined</code>.</li>\n<li><code>a ?? b</code> gives <code>b</code> if <code>a</code> is <code>null</code> or <code>undefined</code>; otherwise <code>a</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>interface Address { city: string; zip?: string }\ninterface Customer { name: string; address?: Address }\n\nconst ada: Customer = { name: &quot;Ada&quot;, address: { city: &quot;London&quot; } };\nconst linus: Customer = { name: &quot;Linus&quot; };\n\nconsole.log(ada.address?.city);              // &quot;London&quot;\nconsole.log(linus.address?.city);            // undefined, no crash\nconsole.log(linus.address?.city ?? &quot;Unknown city&quot;);\nconsole.log(ada.address?.zip ?? &quot;no zip&quot;);\n\nconst count = 0;\nconsole.log(count || 10);   // 10: || treats 0 as missing\nconsole.log(count ?? 10);   // 0: ?? only replaces null/undefined</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>Prefer <code>??</code> over <code>||</code> for default values. <code>||</code> replaces every falsy value, including <code>0</code>, <code>&quot;&quot;</code> and <code>false</code>, which are often perfectly valid.</p>\n<p>These two operators are plain JavaScript (not TypeScript-only), so you can use them anywhere.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"Discriminated unions","html":"<p>A powerful pattern: a union of object types that share one literal property, the <strong>discriminant</strong> (often called <code>kind</code> or <code>type</code>). Checking that property narrows to the right shape.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>type Shape =\n  | { kind: &quot;circle&quot;; radius: number }\n  | { kind: &quot;square&quot;; size: number }\n  | { kind: &quot;rect&quot;; width: number; height: number };\n\nfunction area(shape: Shape): number {\n  switch (shape.kind) {\n    case &quot;circle&quot;:\n      return Math.PI * shape.radius ** 2;\n    case &quot;square&quot;:\n      return shape.size ** 2;\n    case &quot;rect&quot;:\n      return shape.width * shape.height;\n  }\n}\n\nconsole.log(area({ kind: &quot;circle&quot;, radius: 1 }).toFixed(2));\nconsole.log(area({ kind: &quot;rect&quot;, width: 2, height: 5 }));\nconsole.log(area({ kind: &quot;square&quot;, radius: 3 }));   // error: squares have size, not radius</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>Inside <code>case &quot;circle&quot;:</code>, TypeScript knows <code>shape</code> has a <code>radius</code>, and nothing else. Try typing <code>shape.width</code> in that case and you&#39;ll get an error.</p>\n<p>This pattern models a lot of real situations: API responses (<code>success</code> or <code>error</code>), app states (<code>loading</code>, <code>loaded</code>, <code>failed</code>), events, and messages.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"Checking every case","html":"<p>If you later add a new shape, you want TypeScript to tell you every place that needs updating. Assigning to the type <code>never</code> does exactly that.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>type Shape =\n  | { kind: &quot;circle&quot;; radius: number }\n  | { kind: &quot;square&quot;; size: number }\n  | { kind: &quot;triangle&quot;; base: number; height: number };\n\nfunction area(shape: Shape): number {\n  switch (shape.kind) {\n    case &quot;circle&quot;:\n      return Math.PI * shape.radius ** 2;\n    case &quot;square&quot;:\n      return shape.size ** 2;\n    default: {\n      const unhandled: never = shape;   // error: triangle isn't handled\n      throw new Error(&quot;Unknown shape&quot;);\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nconsole.log(area({ kind: &quot;square&quot;, size: 3 }));</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p><code>never</code> is the type of &quot;can&#39;t happen&quot;. If every case is handled, <code>shape</code> in the <code>default</code> branch has type <code>never</code> and the assignment is fine. If one is missing, TypeScript points straight at it. Add a <code>case &quot;triangle&quot;:</code> and the error goes away.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"Type assertions","html":"<p>Sometimes you know more than TypeScript does. <code>value as Type</code> tells the compiler to treat a value as a certain type. It does <strong>no checking at runtime</strong>, so if you&#39;re wrong, things break later.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>const data: unknown = JSON.parse('{&quot;name&quot;: &quot;Ada&quot;}');\n\nconst user = data as { name: string };\nconsole.log(user.name);\n\nconst wrong = data as { age: number };\nconsole.log(wrong.age.toFixed(1));     // compiles, but crashes when run</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>Prefer narrowing (real checks) over assertions. Use <code>as</code> sparingly, for example when you&#39;ve just validated data in a way TypeScript can&#39;t follow. Every <code>as</code> is a promise you make to the compiler, and it&#39;ll believe you.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"quiz","title":"Quick check","question":"<p>What does <code>0 ?? 5</code> give?</p>\n","options":["<code>0</code>","<code>5</code>","<code>undefined</code>","<code>null</code>"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p><code>??</code> only falls back when the left side is <code>null</code> or <code>undefined</code>. <code>0</code> is a real value, so it&#39;s kept. (<code>0 || 5</code> would give 5.)</p>\n"},{"type":"exercise","id":"ts-3-ex1","title":"Format an ID","lang":"ts","starter":"function formatId(id: string | number): string {\n  // Your code here\n\n}\n\nconsole.log(formatId(42));\nconsole.log(formatId(\"ab12\"));","tests":"test(\"42 becomes #0042\", () => expect(formatId(42)).toBe(\"#0042\"));\ntest(\"12345 becomes #12345\", () => expect(formatId(12345)).toBe(\"#12345\"));\ntest('\"ab12\" becomes \"AB12\"', () => expect(formatId(\"ab12\")).toBe(\"AB12\"));","solution":"function formatId(id: string | number): string {\n  if (typeof id === \"number\") {\n    return \"#\" + String(id).padStart(4, \"0\");\n  }\n  return id.toUpperCase();\n}\n\nconsole.log(formatId(42));\nconsole.log(formatId(\"ab12\"));","hints":["<p><code>if (typeof id === &quot;number&quot;) { ... }</code> narrows <code>id</code> to a number inside the block.</p>\n"],"html":"<p>Write <code>formatId(id: string | number): string</code>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a <strong>number</strong> is padded to 4 digits with a <code>#</code>: <code>42</code> becomes <code>&quot;#0042&quot;</code></li>\n<li>a <strong>string</strong> is upper-cased: <code>&quot;ab12&quot;</code> becomes <code>&quot;AB12&quot;</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Use <code>String(n).padStart(4, &quot;0&quot;)</code> to pad.</p>\n"},{"type":"exercise","id":"ts-3-ex2","title":"Move on a grid","lang":"ts","starter":"// type Direction = ...\n\ninterface Position {\n  x: number;\n  y: number;\n}\n\nfunction move(pos: Position, direction: Direction): Position {\n  // Your code here\n\n}\n\nconsole.log(move({ x: 0, y: 0 }, \"up\"));\nconsole.log(move({ x: 2, y: 5 }, \"left\"));","tests":"test(\"up increases y\", () => expect(move({ x: 0, y: 0 }, \"up\")).toEqual({ x: 0, y: 1 }));\ntest(\"down decreases y\", () => expect(move({ x: 0, y: 0 }, \"down\")).toEqual({ x: 0, y: -1 }));\ntest(\"left decreases x\", () => expect(move({ x: 2, y: 5 }, \"left\")).toEqual({ x: 1, y: 5 }));\ntest(\"right increases x\", () => expect(move({ x: 2, y: 5 }, \"right\")).toEqual({ x: 3, y: 5 }));","solution":"type Direction = \"up\" | \"down\" | \"left\" | \"right\";\n\ninterface Position {\n  x: number;\n  y: number;\n}\n\nfunction move(pos: Position, direction: Direction): Position {\n  switch (direction) {\n    case \"up\":\n      return { x: pos.x, y: pos.y + 1 };\n    case \"down\":\n      return { x: pos.x, y: pos.y - 1 };\n    case \"left\":\n      return { x: pos.x - 1, y: pos.y };\n    case \"right\":\n      return { x: pos.x + 1, y: pos.y };\n  }\n}\n\nconsole.log(move({ x: 0, y: 0 }, \"up\"));\nconsole.log(move({ x: 2, y: 5 }, \"left\"));","hints":["<p><code>type Direction = &quot;up&quot; | &quot;down&quot; | &quot;left&quot; | &quot;right&quot;;</code></p>\n","<p>A <code>switch (direction)</code> with four <code>case</code>s, each returning a new <code>{ x, y }</code> object.</p>\n"],"html":"<p>Define a type <code>Direction</code> that only allows <code>&quot;up&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;down&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;left&quot;</code> and <code>&quot;right&quot;</code>. Then finish <code>move</code> so it returns the new position. <code>&quot;up&quot;</code> makes <code>y</code> one bigger, <code>&quot;right&quot;</code> makes <code>x</code> one bigger.</p>\n"},{"type":"exercise","id":"ts-3-ex3","title":"Areas of shapes","lang":"ts","starter":"type Shape =\n  | { kind: \"circle\"; radius: number }\n  | { kind: \"rect\"; width: number; height: number }\n  | { kind: \"triangle\"; base: number; height: number };\n\nfunction area(shape: Shape): number {\n  // Your code here\n\n}\n\nconsole.log(area({ kind: \"rect\", width: 3, height: 4 }));\nconsole.log(area({ kind: \"triangle\", base: 10, height: 3 }));","tests":"test(\"circle with radius 1\", () => expect(area({ kind: \"circle\", radius: 1 })).toBeCloseTo(Math.PI, 4));\ntest(\"3 x 4 rectangle\", () => expect(area({ kind: \"rect\", width: 3, height: 4 })).toBe(12));\ntest(\"triangle 10 x 3\", () => expect(area({ kind: \"triangle\", base: 10, height: 3 })).toBe(15));","solution":"type Shape =\n  | { kind: \"circle\"; radius: number }\n  | { kind: \"rect\"; width: number; height: number }\n  | { kind: \"triangle\"; base: number; height: number };\n\nfunction area(shape: Shape): number {\n  switch (shape.kind) {\n    case \"circle\":\n      return Math.PI * shape.radius ** 2;\n    case \"rect\":\n      return shape.width * shape.height;\n    case \"triangle\":\n      return (shape.base * shape.height) / 2;\n  }\n}\n\nconsole.log(area({ kind: \"rect\", width: 3, height: 4 }));\nconsole.log(area({ kind: \"triangle\", base: 10, height: 3 }));","hints":["<p><code>switch (shape.kind) { case &quot;circle&quot;: ... }</code>. Inside each case, TypeScript knows which properties exist.</p>\n"],"html":"<p>Finish <code>area</code> for this discriminated union. A triangle&#39;s area is <code>base * height / 2</code>.</p>\n"},{"type":"exercise","id":"ts-3-ex4","title":"Find the city","lang":"ts","starter":"interface Customer {\n  name: string;\n  address?: {\n    street?: string;\n    city?: string;\n  };\n}\n\nfunction getCity(customer: Customer): string {\n  // Your code here\n\n}\n\nconsole.log(getCity({ name: \"Ada\", address: { city: \"London\" } }));\nconsole.log(getCity({ name: \"Linus\" }));","tests":"test(\"returns the city when there is one\", () => expect(getCity({ name: \"Ada\", address: { city: \"London\" } })).toBe(\"London\"));\ntest(\"Unknown when there is no address\", () => expect(getCity({ name: \"Linus\" })).toBe(\"Unknown\"));\ntest(\"Unknown when the address has no city\", () => expect(getCity({ name: \"Grace\", address: { street: \"Main St\" } })).toBe(\"Unknown\"));","solution":"interface Customer {\n  name: string;\n  address?: {\n    street?: string;\n    city?: string;\n  };\n}\n\nfunction getCity(customer: Customer): string {\n  return customer.address?.city ?? \"Unknown\";\n}\n\nconsole.log(getCity({ name: \"Ada\", address: { city: \"London\" } }));\nconsole.log(getCity({ name: \"Linus\" }));","hints":["<p><code>customer.address?.city</code> is either a string or <code>undefined</code>.</p>\n","<p>Add <code>?? &quot;Unknown&quot;</code> to supply the fallback.</p>\n"],"html":"<p>Write <code>getCity(customer: Customer): string</code> that returns the customer&#39;s city, or <code>&quot;Unknown&quot;</code> if there is no address or no city. Use <code>?.</code> and <code>??</code>; no <code>if</code> needed.</p>\n"}]},{"id":"ts-4","week":4,"session":1,"lang":"ts","title":"Classes","minutes":60,"summary":"Build objects from classes, hide internal details with <code>private</code>, and share behaviour with inheritance and interfaces.","goals":["Write a class with a constructor, fields and methods","Use <code>private</code>, <code>readonly</code> and parameter properties","Add getters","Extend a class and override methods","Make a class <code>implement</code> an interface"],"sections":[{"type":"step","title":"Classes are blueprints","html":"<p>A <strong>class</strong> is a blueprint for creating objects that share the same shape and behaviour. Classes are plain JavaScript; TypeScript adds types to them.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>class Dog {\n  name: string;\n  tricks: string[] = [];\n\n  constructor(name: string) {\n    this.name = name;\n  }\n\n  learn(trick: string): void {\n    this.tricks.push(trick);\n  }\n\n  describe(): string {\n    return `${this.name} knows ${this.tricks.length} tricks`;\n  }\n}\n\nconst rex = new Dog(&quot;Rex&quot;);\nrex.learn(&quot;sit&quot;);\nrex.learn(&quot;roll over&quot;);\nconsole.log(rex.describe());\n\nconst fido = new Dog(&quot;Fido&quot;);\nconsole.log(fido.describe());\nconsole.log(rex);</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Fields</strong> (<code>name</code>, <code>tricks</code>) are the data each object holds. In TypeScript you declare them at the top of the class.</li>\n<li>The <strong>constructor</strong> runs once when you write <code>new Dog(...)</code>. It sets up the new object.</li>\n<li><strong>Methods</strong> (<code>learn</code>, <code>describe</code>) are functions every Dog has.</li>\n<li>Inside the class, <code>this</code> means &quot;the object this method was called on&quot;.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Each object made with <code>new</code> is an <strong>instance</strong>. <code>rex</code> and <code>fido</code> are separate instances with their own data.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"private and readonly","html":"<p>TypeScript lets you control who can touch what:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>private</code>: only code inside the class can use it</li>\n<li><code>readonly</code>: can only be set in the constructor</li>\n<li><code>public</code>: anyone can use it (the default)</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>class BankAccount {\n  readonly owner: string;\n  private balance = 0;\n\n  constructor(owner: string) {\n    this.owner = owner;\n  }\n\n  deposit(amount: number): void {\n    if (amount &lt;= 0) throw new Error(&quot;Deposit must be positive&quot;);\n    this.balance += amount;\n  }\n\n  getBalance(): number {\n    return this.balance;\n  }\n}\n\nconst account = new BankAccount(&quot;Ada&quot;);\naccount.deposit(100);\nconsole.log(account.getBalance());\n\naccount.balance = 1_000_000;   // error: balance is private\naccount.owner = &quot;Linus&quot;;       // error: owner is readonly</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>Hiding <code>balance</code> means the only way to change it is through <code>deposit</code>, which checks the amount. The class protects its own rules. This idea is called <strong>encapsulation</strong>.</p>\n<p>TypeScript&#39;s <code>private</code> is only checked at compile time. JavaScript also has truly private fields written with <code>#</code>, like <code>#balance</code>, which are enforced while the program runs too. Both are fine to use.</p>\n<p><code>1_000_000</code> is just <code>1000000</code> with underscores for readability.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"Parameter properties","html":"<p>Declaring a field and then assigning it in the constructor is so common that TypeScript has a shortcut: put <code>private</code>, <code>public</code> or <code>readonly</code> in front of a constructor parameter.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>class Point {\n  constructor(\n    public readonly x: number,\n    public readonly y: number,\n  ) {}\n\n  distanceTo(other: Point): number {\n    return Math.hypot(this.x - other.x, this.y - other.y);\n  }\n}\n\nconst a = new Point(0, 0);\nconst b = new Point(3, 4);\nconsole.log(a.distanceTo(b));\nconsole.log(b.x, b.y);</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p><code>public readonly x: number</code> in the constructor both declares the field and assigns it. The empty <code>{}</code> body is fine, because there&#39;s nothing else to do.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"Getters","html":"<p>A <strong>getter</strong> looks like a property from the outside but is calculated by a method. Write <code>get</code> in front of the method.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>class Rectangle {\n  constructor(public width: number, public height: number) {}\n\n  get area(): number {\n    return this.width * this.height;\n  }\n\n  get isSquare(): boolean {\n    return this.width === this.height;\n  }\n}\n\nconst r = new Rectangle(3, 4);\nconsole.log(r.area);        // no brackets: it reads like a property\nr.width = 4;\nconsole.log(r.area, r.isSquare);</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>There&#39;s a matching <code>set</code> for setters, which run code when a property is assigned. Getters are handy for values that are always derived from other values, so they can never get out of sync.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"quiz","title":"Quick check","question":"<p>A field is marked <code>private</code>. Where can it be used?</p>\n","options":["Anywhere","Only inside the class&#39;s own methods","Only in the constructor","Nowhere; it&#39;s hidden completely"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p><code>private</code> members can be used by code inside the class, but not from outside it.</p>\n"},{"type":"step","title":"Inheritance with extends","html":"<p>A class can <strong>extend</strong> another, taking all its fields and methods. It can add new ones, and <strong>override</strong> existing ones with its own version.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>class Animal {\n  constructor(public name: string) {}\n\n  speak(): string {\n    return `${this.name} makes a sound`;\n  }\n}\n\nclass Cat extends Animal {\n  constructor(name: string, public indoor: boolean) {\n    super(name);   // run Animal's constructor first\n  }\n\n  speak(): string {\n    return `${this.name} says meow`;\n  }\n}\n\nconst animals: Animal[] = [new Animal(&quot;Generic&quot;), new Cat(&quot;Tom&quot;, true)];\nfor (const a of animals) {\n  console.log(a.speak());\n}</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><ul>\n<li><code>super(...)</code> calls the parent class&#39;s constructor. It&#39;s required in a child constructor, before you use <code>this</code>.</li>\n<li><code>super.speak()</code> would call the parent&#39;s version of a method, if you need it.</li>\n<li>A <code>Cat</code> can be used wherever an <code>Animal</code> is expected.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Inheritance is easy to overuse. Deep chains of classes extending classes become hard to change. One level, or using interfaces instead, is usually enough.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"Implementing an interface","html":"<p><code>implements</code> makes TypeScript check that a class provides everything an interface describes. Different classes can implement the same interface in completely different ways.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>interface Storage {\n  save(key: string, value: string): void;\n  load(key: string): string | undefined;\n}\n\nclass MemoryStorage implements Storage {\n  private data = new Map&lt;string, string&gt;();\n\n  save(key: string, value: string): void {\n    this.data.set(key, value);\n  }\n\n  load(key: string): string | undefined {\n    return this.data.get(key);\n  }\n}\n\nclass LoudStorage implements Storage {\n  save(key: string, value: string): void {\n    console.log(`SAVING ${key}=${value}`);\n  }\n  // error: load is missing\n}\n\nconst store: Storage = new MemoryStorage();\nstore.save(&quot;theme&quot;, &quot;dark&quot;);\nconsole.log(store.load(&quot;theme&quot;));</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p><code>Map</code> is a built-in key/value collection, like an object used as a dictionary but with handy methods (<code>set</code>, <code>get</code>, <code>has</code>, <code>delete</code>, <code>size</code>). <code>new Map&lt;string, string&gt;()</code> says both keys and values are strings.</p>\n<p>Code that only depends on the <code>Storage</code> interface doesn&#39;t care which class it gets. You could swap <code>MemoryStorage</code> for one that saves to a database without changing anything else.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"Static members","html":"<p><code>static</code> members belong to the class itself, not to each instance.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>class Temperature {\n  static readonly FREEZING_C = 0;\n\n  constructor(public celsius: number) {}\n\n  static fromFahrenheit(f: number): Temperature {\n    return new Temperature(((f - 32) * 5) / 9);\n  }\n\n  get isFreezing(): boolean {\n    return this.celsius &lt;= Temperature.FREEZING_C;\n  }\n}\n\nconst t = Temperature.fromFahrenheit(212);\nconsole.log(t.celsius, t.isFreezing);\nconsole.log(Temperature.FREEZING_C);</code></pre></div>\n"},{"type":"step","title":"Classes or plain objects?","html":"<p>In TypeScript you often don&#39;t need a class. Plain objects described by an interface, plus functions that work on them, cover most needs.</p>\n<p>Reach for a class when:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>an object has <strong>internal state</strong> that must follow rules (like the bank balance)</li>\n<li>you&#39;ll create <strong>many instances</strong> that share behaviour</li>\n<li>a library or framework expects classes</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>// Plain data + functions: very common in TypeScript code\ninterface Todo { id: number; title: string; done: boolean }\n\nconst complete = (todo: Todo): Todo =&gt; ({ ...todo, done: true });\n\nconst t: Todo = { id: 1, title: &quot;Learn classes&quot;, done: false };\nconsole.log(complete(t));</code></pre></div>\n"},{"type":"quiz","title":"Quick check","question":"<p>In a child class&#39;s constructor, what must you call before using <code>this</code>?</p>\n","options":["<code>this.constructor()</code>","<code>super(...)</code>","<code>extends(...)</code>","<code>parent()</code>"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p><code>super(...)</code> runs the parent&#39;s constructor, which sets up the inherited fields.</p>\n"},{"type":"exercise","id":"ts-4-ex1","title":"A bank account","lang":"ts","starter":"class BankAccount {\n  private balance: number;\n\n  constructor(initial: number) {\n    this.balance = initial;\n  }\n\n  deposit(amount: number): void {\n    // Your code here\n  }\n\n  withdraw(amount: number): void {\n    // Your code here\n  }\n\n  getBalance(): number {\n    return this.balance;\n  }\n}\n\nconst acc = new BankAccount(100);\nacc.deposit(50);\nacc.withdraw(30);\nconsole.log(acc.getBalance());","tests":"test(\"deposit adds money\", () => {\n  const a = new BankAccount(100);\n  a.deposit(50);\n  expect(a.getBalance()).toBe(150);\n});\ntest(\"withdraw takes money out\", () => {\n  const a = new BankAccount(100);\n  a.withdraw(40);\n  expect(a.getBalance()).toBe(60);\n});\ntest(\"withdrawing too much throws 'Insufficient funds'\", () => {\n  const a = new BankAccount(10);\n  expect(() => a.withdraw(20)).toThrow(\"Insufficient funds\");\n});\ntest(\"a failed withdrawal leaves the balance alone\", () => {\n  const a = new BankAccount(10);\n  try { a.withdraw(20); } catch (e) {}\n  expect(a.getBalance()).toBe(10);\n});","solution":"class BankAccount {\n  private balance: number;\n\n  constructor(initial: number) {\n    this.balance = initial;\n  }\n\n  deposit(amount: number): void {\n    this.balance += amount;\n  }\n\n  withdraw(amount: number): void {\n    if (amount > this.balance) {\n      throw new Error(\"Insufficient funds\");\n    }\n    this.balance -= amount;\n  }\n\n  getBalance(): number {\n    return this.balance;\n  }\n}\n\nconst acc = new BankAccount(100);\nacc.deposit(50);\nacc.withdraw(30);\nconsole.log(acc.getBalance());","hints":["<p>Use <code>this.balance += amount;</code> and <code>this.balance -= amount;</code>.</p>\n","<p>Check <code>if (amount &gt; this.balance)</code> <strong>before</strong> subtracting.</p>\n"],"html":"<p>Finish the <code>BankAccount</code> class:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>deposit(amount)</code> adds to the balance</li>\n<li><code>withdraw(amount)</code> subtracts from the balance, but <strong>throws</strong> an <code>Error</code> with the message <code>&quot;Insufficient funds&quot;</code> if there isn&#39;t enough money</li>\n<li><code>balance</code> must stay <code>private</code>, so the class exposes a <code>getBalance()</code> method</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"type":"exercise","id":"ts-4-ex2","title":"A temperature with getters","lang":"ts","starter":"// class Temperature ...\n\n\nconst boiling = new Temperature(100);\nconsole.log(boiling.fahrenheit, boiling.kelvin);","tests":"test(\"100°C is 212°F\", () => expect(new Temperature(100).fahrenheit).toBe(212));\ntest(\"0°C is 273.15 K\", () => expect(new Temperature(0).kelvin).toBeCloseTo(273.15));\ntest(\"fahrenheit follows changes to celsius\", () => {\n  const t = new Temperature(0);\n  t.celsius = 100;\n  expect(t.fahrenheit).toBe(212);\n});","solution":"class Temperature {\n  constructor(public celsius: number) {}\n\n  get fahrenheit(): number {\n    return (this.celsius * 9) / 5 + 32;\n  }\n\n  get kelvin(): number {\n    return this.celsius + 273.15;\n  }\n}\n\nconst boiling = new Temperature(100);\nconsole.log(boiling.fahrenheit, boiling.kelvin);","hints":["<p><code>constructor(public celsius: number) {}</code> declares and sets the field in one go.</p>\n","<p><code>get fahrenheit(): number { return ...; }</code></p>\n"],"html":"<p>Write a class <code>Temperature</code>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the constructor takes <code>celsius: number</code> (use a parameter property)</li>\n<li>a getter <code>fahrenheit</code> returns <code>celsius * 9 / 5 + 32</code></li>\n<li>a getter <code>kelvin</code> returns <code>celsius + 273.15</code></li>\n</ul>\n"},{"type":"exercise","id":"ts-4-ex3","title":"Animals that speak","lang":"ts","starter":"class Animal {\n  constructor(public name: string) {}\n\n  speak(): string {\n    return `${this.name} makes a sound`;\n  }\n}\n\n// class Duck ...\n\n\nconst donald = new Duck(\"Donald\");\nconsole.log(donald.speak());\nconsole.log(donald.fly());","tests":"test(\"a Duck says quack\", () => expect(new Duck(\"Donald\").speak()).toBe(\"Donald says quack\"));\ntest(\"a Duck can fly\", () => expect(new Duck(\"Daisy\").fly()).toBe(\"Daisy flies away\"));\ntest(\"a Duck is an Animal\", () => expect(new Duck(\"X\")).toBeInstanceOf(Animal));\ntest(\"Animal still makes a sound\", () => expect(new Animal(\"Cow\").speak()).toBe(\"Cow makes a sound\"));","solution":"class Animal {\n  constructor(public name: string) {}\n\n  speak(): string {\n    return `${this.name} makes a sound`;\n  }\n}\n\nclass Duck extends Animal {\n  speak(): string {\n    return `${this.name} says quack`;\n  }\n\n  fly(): string {\n    return `${this.name} flies away`;\n  }\n}\n\nconst donald = new Duck(\"Donald\");\nconsole.log(donald.speak());\nconsole.log(donald.fly());","hints":["<p><code>class Duck extends Animal { ... }</code>. You don&#39;t need a constructor, because Duck uses Animal&#39;s.</p>\n"],"html":"<p>Create a class <code>Duck</code> that <strong>extends</strong> <code>Animal</code> and overrides <code>speak()</code> so it returns <code>&quot;&lt;name&gt; says quack&quot;</code>. Also give <code>Duck</code> a new method <code>fly()</code> that returns <code>&quot;&lt;name&gt; flies away&quot;</code>.</p>\n"},{"type":"exercise","id":"ts-4-ex4","title":"Implement an interface","lang":"ts","starter":"type Level = \"info\" | \"warn\" | \"error\";\n\ninterface Logger {\n  log(level: Level, text: string): void;\n  count(): number;\n}\n\n// class MemoryLogger implements Logger ...\n\n\nconst logger = new MemoryLogger();\nlogger.log(\"info\", \"App started\");\nlogger.log(\"warn\", \"Disk almost full\");\nconsole.log(logger.count());\nconsole.log(logger.messages);","tests":"test(\"starts empty\", () => expect(new MemoryLogger().count()).toBe(0));\ntest(\"stores formatted messages\", () => {\n  const l = new MemoryLogger();\n  l.log(\"warn\", \"Disk almost full\");\n  expect(l.messages).toEqual([\"[warn] Disk almost full\"]);\n});\ntest(\"count() counts messages\", () => {\n  const l = new MemoryLogger();\n  l.log(\"info\", \"a\");\n  l.log(\"error\", \"b\");\n  expect(l.count()).toBe(2);\n});","solution":"type Level = \"info\" | \"warn\" | \"error\";\n\ninterface Logger {\n  log(level: Level, text: string): void;\n  count(): number;\n}\n\nclass MemoryLogger implements Logger {\n  messages: string[] = [];\n\n  log(level: Level, text: string): void {\n    this.messages.push(`[${level}] ${text}`);\n  }\n\n  count(): number {\n    return this.messages.length;\n  }\n}\n\nconst logger = new MemoryLogger();\nlogger.log(\"info\", \"App started\");\nlogger.log(\"warn\", \"Disk almost full\");\nconsole.log(logger.count());\nconsole.log(logger.messages);","hints":["<p><code>class MemoryLogger implements Logger { messages: string[] = []; ... }</code></p>\n","<p><code>log</code> pushes a template string into <code>this.messages</code>; <code>count</code> returns <code>this.messages.length</code>.</p>\n"],"html":"<p>Write a class <code>MemoryLogger</code> that <strong>implements</strong> the <code>Logger</code> interface. It should keep every message in an array called <code>messages</code>, and <code>count()</code> should return how many messages were logged. <code>log</code> should store the message as <code>&quot;[level] text&quot;</code>, for example <code>&quot;[warn] Disk almost full&quot;</code>.</p>\n"}]},{"id":"ts-5","week":4,"session":2,"lang":"ts","title":"Generics and utility types","minutes":60,"summary":"Write functions and types that work with any type while keeping full type safety, and reshape types with built-in helpers.","goals":["Write generic functions with type parameters","Build generic interfaces and classes","Limit generics with <code>extends</code> constraints","Use <code>keyof</code> to work with property names","Use <code>Partial</code>, <code>Pick</code>, <code>Omit</code>, <code>Readonly</code> and <code>Record</code>"],"sections":[{"type":"step","title":"The problem generics solve","html":"<p>Say you want a function that returns the first item of any array. How do you type it?</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>function firstAny(items: any[]): any {\n  return items[0];\n}\n\nconst n = firstAny([1, 2, 3]);\nn.toUpperCase();     // no error... but n is a number. Crash!</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>Using <code>any</code> works, but you lose all type information. TypeScript no longer knows that the first item of a <code>number[]</code> is a <code>number</code>, so it can&#39;t protect you. Writing one function per type (<code>firstNumber</code>, <code>firstString</code>, ...) would be silly.</p>\n<p><strong>Generics</strong> fix this.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"Your first generic function","html":"<p>A <strong>type parameter</strong>, written in angle brackets like <code>&lt;T&gt;</code>, is a placeholder for a type. It gets filled in each time the function is called.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>function first&lt;T&gt;(items: T[]): T | undefined {\n  return items[0];\n}\n\nconst n = first([1, 2, 3]);          // T is number\nconst s = first([&quot;a&quot;, &quot;b&quot;]);         // T is string\nconst nothing = first&lt;boolean&gt;([]);  // you can also say T explicitly\n\nconsole.log(n, s, nothing);\ns?.toUpperCase();     // fine\nn?.toUpperCase();     // error: numbers don't have toUpperCase</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>Read <code>first&lt;T&gt;(items: T[]): T | undefined</code> as: &quot;for some type <code>T</code>, take an array of <code>T</code> and return a <code>T</code> (or undefined, if the array is empty)&quot;.</p>\n<p>You almost never have to write the type yourself when calling. TypeScript <strong>infers</strong> <code>T</code> from the arguments.</p>\n<p><code>T</code> is just a convention, short for &quot;Type&quot;. You can use any name, and longer names like <code>TItem</code> help when there are several.</p>\n<p>You&#39;ve already been using generics: <code>Array&lt;number&gt;</code>, <code>Promise&lt;string&gt;</code>, <code>Map&lt;string, number&gt;</code> and <code>Record&lt;string, number&gt;</code> are all generic types.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"More than one type parameter","html":"<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>function pair&lt;A, B&gt;(a: A, b: B): [A, B] {\n  return [a, b];\n}\n\nfunction mapValues&lt;T, U&gt;(items: T[], fn: (item: T) =&gt; U): U[] {\n  const result: U[] = [];\n  for (const item of items) {\n    result.push(fn(item));\n  }\n  return result;\n}\n\nconst p = pair(&quot;age&quot;, 36);             // [string, number]\nconst lengths = mapValues([&quot;hi&quot;, &quot;hello&quot;], (s) =&gt; s.length);   // number[]\nconsole.log(p, lengths);</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p><code>mapValues</code> is basically how <code>Array.prototype.map</code> is typed. In the call, TypeScript works out <code>T = string</code> from the array, then works out <code>U = number</code> from what your arrow function returns.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"Generic interfaces and types","html":"<p>Types can have type parameters too. This is how you describe &quot;a box of something&quot; or &quot;an API response containing some data&quot;.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>interface ApiResponse&lt;T&gt; {\n  ok: boolean;\n  data: T;\n  fetchedAt: string;\n}\n\ninterface User { id: number; name: string }\n\nconst userResponse: ApiResponse&lt;User&gt; = {\n  ok: true,\n  data: { id: 1, name: &quot;Ada&quot; },\n  fetchedAt: &quot;2026-01-01&quot;,\n};\n\nconst listResponse: ApiResponse&lt;string[]&gt; = {\n  ok: true,\n  data: [&quot;a&quot;, &quot;b&quot;],\n  fetchedAt: &quot;2026-01-01&quot;,\n};\n\nconsole.log(userResponse.data.name);\nconsole.log(listResponse.data.length);</code></pre></div>\n"},{"type":"step","title":"Generic classes","html":"<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>class Stack&lt;T&gt; {\n  private items: T[] = [];\n\n  push(item: T): void {\n    this.items.push(item);\n  }\n\n  pop(): T | undefined {\n    return this.items.pop();\n  }\n\n  get size(): number {\n    return this.items.length;\n  }\n}\n\nconst numbers = new Stack&lt;number&gt;();\nnumbers.push(1);\nnumbers.push(2);\nconsole.log(numbers.pop(), numbers.size);\nnumbers.push(&quot;three&quot;);   // error: this is a stack of numbers</code></pre></div>\n"},{"type":"quiz","title":"Quick check","question":"<p><code>function wrap&lt;T&gt;(value: T): T[] { return [value]; }</code>. What is the type of <code>wrap(&quot;hi&quot;)</code>?</p>\n","options":["<code>T[]</code>","<code>any[]</code>","<code>string[]</code>","<code>string</code>"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>TypeScript infers <code>T = string</code> from the argument, so the return type <code>T[]</code> becomes <code>string[]</code>.</p>\n"},{"type":"step","title":"Constraints with extends","html":"<p>Sometimes <code>T</code> can&#39;t be just anything. You need it to have certain properties. Use <code>extends</code> to set a <strong>constraint</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>function longest&lt;T extends { length: number }&gt;(a: T, b: T): T {\n  return a.length &gt;= b.length ? a : b;\n}\n\nconsole.log(longest(&quot;apple&quot;, &quot;kiwi&quot;));     // strings have length\nconsole.log(longest([1, 2], [1, 2, 3]));   // arrays have length\nconsole.log(longest(10, 20));              // error: numbers don't have length</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>Without the constraint, <code>a.length</code> would be an error. TypeScript can&#39;t assume an arbitrary <code>T</code> has a <code>length</code>. The constraint promises that it does.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"keyof and indexed access","html":"<p><code>keyof T</code> is the union of a type&#39;s property names. <code>T[K]</code> is the type of the property <code>K</code>. Together they let you write safe functions that work with property names.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>interface Book {\n  title: string;\n  pages: number;\n  isRead: boolean;\n}\n\ntype BookKey = keyof Book;      // &quot;title&quot; | &quot;pages&quot; | &quot;isRead&quot;\n\nfunction getProp&lt;T, K extends keyof T&gt;(obj: T, key: K): T[K] {\n  return obj[key];\n}\n\nconst book: Book = { title: &quot;Dune&quot;, pages: 412, isRead: false };\n\nconst pages = getProp(book, &quot;pages&quot;);     // number\nconst title = getProp(book, &quot;title&quot;);     // string\nconsole.log(pages + 1, title.toUpperCase());\ngetProp(book, &quot;author&quot;);                  // error: not a key of Book</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p><code>K extends keyof T</code> means &quot;K must be one of T&#39;s property names&quot;. The return type <code>T[K]</code> then gives exactly the type of that property. Libraries use this pattern all the time, for example for sorting by a field or picking columns from a table.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"Utility types","html":"<p>TypeScript has built-in generic types that transform other types. These save a lot of repetition.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>interface Todo {\n  id: number;\n  title: string;\n  done: boolean;\n  dueDate: string;\n}\n\ntype TodoUpdate = Partial&lt;Todo&gt;;                   // every property optional\ntype TodoPreview = Pick&lt;Todo, &quot;id&quot; | &quot;title&quot;&gt;;     // only these properties\ntype NewTodo = Omit&lt;Todo, &quot;id&quot;&gt;;                   // everything except id\ntype FrozenTodo = Readonly&lt;Todo&gt;;                  // nothing can be changed\n\nconst changes: TodoUpdate = { done: true };\nconst preview: TodoPreview = { id: 1, title: &quot;Learn generics&quot; };\nconst draft: NewTodo = { title: &quot;Write tests&quot;, done: false, dueDate: &quot;2026-12-01&quot; };\n\nconst frozen: FrozenTodo = { id: 1, title: &quot;Fixed&quot;, done: false, dueDate: &quot;&quot; };\nfrozen.done = true;    // error: readonly\n\nconsole.log(changes, preview, draft);</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>A cheat sheet:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Utility</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Partial&lt;T&gt;</code></td>\n<td>all properties optional</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Required&lt;T&gt;</code></td>\n<td>all properties required</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Readonly&lt;T&gt;</code></td>\n<td>all properties read-only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Pick&lt;T, K&gt;</code></td>\n<td>keep only the keys <code>K</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Omit&lt;T, K&gt;</code></td>\n<td>remove the keys <code>K</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Record&lt;K, V&gt;</code></td>\n<td>an object with keys <code>K</code> and values <code>V</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ReturnType&lt;F&gt;</code></td>\n<td>the return type of function type <code>F</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The big benefit: when <code>Todo</code> changes, all the derived types update automatically.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"quiz","title":"Quick check","question":"<p>Given <code>interface User { id: number; name: string; email: string }</code>, which type is <code>{ name: string; email: string }</code>?</p>\n","options":["<code>Pick&lt;User, &quot;id&quot;&gt;</code>","<code>Omit&lt;User, &quot;id&quot;&gt;</code>","<code>Partial&lt;User&gt;</code>","<code>keyof User</code>"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p><code>Omit</code> removes the listed keys. <code>Pick&lt;User, &quot;name&quot; | &quot;email&quot;&gt;</code> would also work.</p>\n"},{"type":"exercise","id":"ts-5-ex1","title":"The last item","lang":"ts","starter":"// function last ...\n\n\nconst n = last([1, 2, 3]);\nconst s = last([\"a\", \"b\"]);\nconsole.log(n, s, last([]));","tests":"test(\"last([1, 2, 3]) is 3\", () => expect(last([1, 2, 3])).toBe(3));\ntest('last([\"a\", \"b\"]) is \"b\"', () => expect(last([\"a\", \"b\"])).toBe(\"b\"));\ntest(\"last([]) is undefined\", () => expect(last([])).toBe(undefined));","solution":"function last<T>(items: T[]): T | undefined {\n  return items[items.length - 1];\n}\n\nconst n = last([1, 2, 3]);\nconst s = last([\"a\", \"b\"]);\nconsole.log(n, s, last([]));","hints":["<p><code>function last&lt;T&gt;(items: T[]): T | undefined { ... }</code></p>\n","<p>The last index is <code>items.length - 1</code>.</p>\n"],"html":"<p>Write a generic function <code>last&lt;T&gt;(items: T[]): T | undefined</code> that returns the last item of an array, or <code>undefined</code> for an empty array.</p>\n"},{"type":"exercise","id":"ts-5-ex2","title":"A generic queue","lang":"ts","starter":"// class Queue<T> ...\n\n\nconst q = new Queue<string>();\nq.enqueue(\"Ada\");\nq.enqueue(\"Linus\");\nconsole.log(q.peek(), q.size);\nconsole.log(q.dequeue(), q.size);","tests":"test(\"dequeue returns items in the order added\", () => {\n  const q = new Queue();\n  q.enqueue(1); q.enqueue(2); q.enqueue(3);\n  expect(q.dequeue()).toBe(1);\n  expect(q.dequeue()).toBe(2);\n});\ntest(\"peek doesn't remove\", () => {\n  const q = new Queue();\n  q.enqueue(\"a\");\n  q.peek();\n  expect(q.size).toBe(1);\n  expect(q.peek()).toBe(\"a\");\n});\ntest(\"empty queue returns undefined\", () => expect(new Queue().dequeue()).toBe(undefined));\ntest(\"size counts items\", () => {\n  const q = new Queue();\n  q.enqueue(1); q.enqueue(1);\n  expect(q.size).toBe(2);\n});","solution":"class Queue<T> {\n  private items: T[] = [];\n\n  enqueue(item: T): void {\n    this.items.push(item);\n  }\n\n  dequeue(): T | undefined {\n    return this.items.shift();\n  }\n\n  peek(): T | undefined {\n    return this.items[0];\n  }\n\n  get size(): number {\n    return this.items.length;\n  }\n}\n\nconst q = new Queue<string>();\nq.enqueue(\"Ada\");\nq.enqueue(\"Linus\");\nconsole.log(q.peek(), q.size);\nconsole.log(q.dequeue(), q.size);","hints":["<p>Look at the <code>Stack&lt;T&gt;</code> example in this lesson. A queue is the same, except you take items from the <strong>front</strong>.</p>\n","<p><code>this.items.shift()</code> removes and returns the first item.</p>\n"],"html":"<p>Write a generic class <code>Queue&lt;T&gt;</code> (first in, first out) with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>enqueue(item: T): void</code> adds to the back</li>\n<li><code>dequeue(): T | undefined</code> removes and returns the item at the front</li>\n<li><code>peek(): T | undefined</code> returns the front item without removing it</li>\n<li>a getter <code>size</code> returning how many items there are</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"type":"exercise","id":"ts-5-ex3","title":"Update with Partial","lang":"ts","starter":"interface Todo {\n  id: number;\n  title: string;\n  done: boolean;\n}\n\n// type TodoPreview = ...\n\n// function updateTodo ...\n\n// function toPreview ...\n\n\nconst t: Todo = { id: 1, title: \"Learn TypeScript\", done: false };\nconsole.log(updateTodo(t, { done: true }));\nconsole.log(toPreview(t));","tests":"const base = { id: 1, title: \"Learn TypeScript\", done: false };\ntest(\"applies changes\", () => expect(updateTodo(base, { done: true })).toEqual({ id: 1, title: \"Learn TypeScript\", done: true }));\ntest(\"doesn't change the original\", () => {\n  updateTodo(base, { title: \"Other\" });\n  expect(base.title).toBe(\"Learn TypeScript\");\n});\ntest(\"toPreview keeps only id and title\", () => expect(toPreview(base)).toEqual({ id: 1, title: \"Learn TypeScript\" }));","solution":"interface Todo {\n  id: number;\n  title: string;\n  done: boolean;\n}\n\ntype TodoPreview = Pick<Todo, \"id\" | \"title\">;\n\nfunction updateTodo(todo: Todo, changes: Partial<Todo>): Todo {\n  return { ...todo, ...changes };\n}\n\nfunction toPreview(todo: Todo): TodoPreview {\n  return { id: todo.id, title: todo.title };\n}\n\nconst t: Todo = { id: 1, title: \"Learn TypeScript\", done: false };\nconsole.log(updateTodo(t, { done: true }));\nconsole.log(toPreview(t));","hints":["<p><code>type TodoPreview = Pick&lt;Todo, &quot;id&quot; | &quot;title&quot;&gt;;</code></p>\n","<p><code>updateTodo</code> is the same spread trick as in week 2: <code>{ ...todo, ...changes }</code>. In <code>toPreview</code>, build the new object property by property, because spreading would keep <code>done</code> too.</p>\n"],"html":"<p>Write <code>updateTodo(todo: Todo, changes: Partial&lt;Todo&gt;): Todo</code> that returns a new todo with the changes applied. Then write <code>toPreview(todo: Todo): TodoPreview</code>, where <code>TodoPreview</code> is defined with <code>Pick</code> to contain only <code>id</code> and <code>title</code>.</p>\n"},{"type":"exercise","id":"ts-5-ex4","title":"Pluck a column","lang":"ts","starter":"// function pluck ...\n\n\nconst books = [\n  { title: \"Dune\", pages: 412 },\n  { title: \"Emma\", pages: 474 },\n];\nconst titles = pluck(books, \"title\");\nconst pages = pluck(books, \"pages\");\nconsole.log(titles, pages);","tests":"const bs = [{ title: \"Dune\", pages: 412 }, { title: \"Emma\", pages: 474 }];\ntest(\"plucks titles\", () => expect(pluck(bs, \"title\")).toEqual([\"Dune\", \"Emma\"]));\ntest(\"plucks pages\", () => expect(pluck(bs, \"pages\")).toEqual([412, 474]));\ntest(\"empty array gives []\", () => expect(pluck([], \"x\")).toEqual([]));","solution":"function pluck<T, K extends keyof T>(items: T[], key: K): T[K][] {\n  return items.map((item) => item[key]);\n}\n\nconst books = [\n  { title: \"Dune\", pages: 412 },\n  { title: \"Emma\", pages: 474 },\n];\nconst titles = pluck(books, \"title\");\nconst pages = pluck(books, \"pages\");\nconsole.log(titles, pages);","hints":["<p>The signature is given in the instructions. Copy it exactly.</p>\n","<p>The body is a single <code>map</code>: <code>items.map((item) =&gt; item[key])</code>.</p>\n"],"html":"<p>Write <code>pluck&lt;T, K extends keyof T&gt;(items: T[], key: K): T[K][]</code> that returns the value of one property from every item.</p>\n<p><code>pluck(books, &quot;title&quot;)</code> should give an array of titles, typed as <code>string[]</code>.</p>\n"}]},{"id":"ts-6","week":4,"session":3,"lang":"ts","title":"Async TypeScript and the final project","minutes":75,"summary":"Type asynchronous code and errors, then build a small typed task tracker that uses everything from the month.","goals":["Type Promises and <code>async</code> functions","Describe API data with interfaces","Handle errors typed as <code>unknown</code>","Use a <code>Result</code> type instead of throwing","Build and test a complete typed program"],"sections":[{"type":"step","title":"Typing Promises","html":"<p>An <code>async</code> function always returns a Promise. In TypeScript you write that as <code>Promise&lt;T&gt;</code>, where <code>T</code> is the type of the value it resolves to.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>const wait = (ms: number): Promise&lt;void&gt; =&gt;\n  new Promise((resolve) =&gt; setTimeout(resolve, ms));\n\nasync function getGreeting(name: string): Promise&lt;string&gt; {\n  await wait(200);\n  return `Hello, ${name}`;\n}\n\nconst greeting = await getGreeting(&quot;Ada&quot;);   // string\nconsole.log(greeting.toUpperCase());\n\nconst notAwaited = getGreeting(&quot;Linus&quot;);     // Promise&lt;string&gt;\nconsole.log(notAwaited.toUpperCase());       // error: forgot to await!</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>That second error is a real lifesaver. Forgetting <code>await</code> is one of the most common async bugs in JavaScript, and TypeScript catches it because a <code>Promise&lt;string&gt;</code> isn&#39;t a <code>string</code>.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"Typing data from an API","html":"<p>Data from the network arrives as JSON with no type information. You describe what you <strong>expect</strong> with an interface. Here <code>fakeFetch</code> stands in for a real API.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>interface Post {\n  id: number;\n  title: string;\n  likes: number;\n  tags: string[];\n}\n\nconst database: Post[] = [\n  { id: 1, title: &quot;Why types?&quot;, likes: 42, tags: [&quot;ts&quot;] },\n  { id: 2, title: &quot;Async in 5 minutes&quot;, likes: 17, tags: [&quot;js&quot;, &quot;async&quot;] },\n];\n\nasync function fakeFetch(url: string): Promise&lt;string&gt; {\n  await new Promise((r) =&gt; setTimeout(r, 200));\n  return JSON.stringify(database);\n}\n\nasync function getPosts(): Promise&lt;Post[]&gt; {\n  const text = await fakeFetch(&quot;/api/posts&quot;);\n  return JSON.parse(text) as Post[];\n}\n\nconst posts = await getPosts();\nconst popular = posts.filter((p) =&gt; p.likes &gt; 20).map((p) =&gt; p.title);\nconsole.log(popular);</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p><code>JSON.parse</code> returns <code>any</code>, and <code>as Post[]</code> tells TypeScript to trust you. It doesn&#39;t check anything. If the server sends a different shape, you won&#39;t find out until something breaks.</p>\n<p>In real projects people use <strong>validation libraries</strong> such as Zod or Valibot. You describe the shape once; the library checks the data while the program runs and gives you a TypeScript type for free. That&#39;s a great next step after this course.</p>\n<p>With the real browser <code>fetch</code> it looks like:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\" data-mode=\"static\"><pre><code>async function getPosts(): Promise&lt;Post[]&gt; {\n  const response = await fetch(&quot;https://example.com/api/posts&quot;);\n  if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}`);\n  return (await response.json()) as Post[];\n}</code></pre></div>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"Errors are unknown","html":"<p>In a <code>catch</code> block, the error has type <code>unknown</code>, because JavaScript lets you throw <strong>anything</strong>, not only <code>Error</code> objects. Narrow it before you use it.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>function getErrorMessage(error: unknown): string {\n  if (error instanceof Error) {\n    return error.message;\n  }\n  if (typeof error === &quot;string&quot;) {\n    return error;\n  }\n  return &quot;Something went wrong&quot;;\n}\n\ntry {\n  JSON.parse(&quot;{ broken&quot;);\n} catch (error) {\n  console.log(error.message);              // error: 'error' is of type 'unknown'\n  console.log(getErrorMessage(error));     // fine\n}\n\ntry {\n  throw &quot;a plain string&quot;;\n} catch (error) {\n  console.log(getErrorMessage(error));\n}</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>A small helper like <code>getErrorMessage</code> is worth keeping in every project.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"Returning results instead of throwing","html":"<p>Throwing hides failure: nothing in a function&#39;s type says it might throw. An alternative is to <strong>return</strong> success or failure as a discriminated union. Then TypeScript forces the caller to handle both.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\" data-mode=\"run\"><pre><code>type Result&lt;T&gt; =\n  | { ok: true; value: T }\n  | { ok: false; error: string };\n\nfunction parseAge(input: string): Result&lt;number&gt; {\n  const age = Number(input);\n  if (Number.isNaN(age)) return { ok: false, error: `&quot;${input}&quot; is not a number` };\n  if (age &lt; 0 || age &gt; 150) return { ok: false, error: &quot;Age out of range&quot; };\n  return { ok: true, value: age };\n}\n\nfor (const input of [&quot;36&quot;, &quot;abc&quot;, &quot;-4&quot;]) {\n  const result = parseAge(input);\n  if (result.ok) {\n    console.log(&quot;Age:&quot;, result.value);\n  } else {\n    console.log(&quot;Problem:&quot;, result.error);\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>This combines three things from this course: a generic type (<code>Result&lt;T&gt;</code>), a discriminated union (on <code>ok</code>) and narrowing. You&#39;ll see this pattern in many TypeScript codebases. Both styles are fine; use throwing for truly unexpected problems and <code>Result</code> for failures that are a normal part of the job, like invalid user input.</p>\n</div>"},{"type":"quiz","title":"Quick check","question":"<p><code>async function load(): Promise&lt;number[]&gt; { ... }</code>. What is the type of <code>load()</code> <strong>without</strong> <code>await</code>?</p>\n","options":["<code>number[]</code>","<code>Promise&lt;number[]&gt;</code>","<code>number</code>","<code>void</code>"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Calling an async function gives you a Promise. You need <code>await load()</code> to get the <code>number[]</code>.</p>\n"},{"type":"step","title":"The final project","html":"<p>The last two exercises are a small <strong>task tracker</strong>, built in two parts. It uses almost everything from the month:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>interfaces</strong> and <strong>literal union types</strong> for the data</li>\n<li>a <strong>class</strong> with <strong>private</strong> state</li>\n<li><strong>array methods</strong> (<code>filter</code>, <code>map</code>, <code>find</code>, <code>reduce</code>, <code>sort</code>)</li>\n<li><strong>optional</strong> properties and <strong><code>Partial</code></strong></li>\n<li><strong>errors</strong> for invalid actions</li>\n<li>a <strong><code>Record</code></strong> for counting</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Work through part 1 first. When all its checks pass, move to part 2, which starts from a finished part 1.</p>\n\n<div class=\"only-guide\"><p>Tips for working on a bigger exercise:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Read every requirement before writing code.</li>\n<li>Implement one method at a time and press <strong>Run</strong> often. Use <code>console.log</code> to look at what you&#39;ve got.</li>\n<li>Press <strong>Check answer</strong> early. The failing checks tell you what&#39;s left.</li>\n<li>Hints are fine. So is looking at the solution, as long as you then rebuild it yourself without peeking.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>"},{"type":"step","title":"Where to go next","html":"<p>After this month you know enough to start real projects. Good next steps:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Set up TypeScript locally.</strong> Install Node.js and VS Code, then run <code>npm create vite@latest</code> and pick the &quot;Vanilla + TypeScript&quot; template. You get a real project with <code>tsconfig.json</code>, instant type errors in the editor, and a browser preview.</li>\n<li><strong>Read the TypeScript Handbook</strong> at typescriptlang.org/docs. You&#39;ll recognise most of it now.</li>\n<li><strong>Build something small you care about</strong>: a budget tracker, a quiz, a habit counter. Building teaches more than reading.</li>\n<li><strong>Pick a framework</strong> when you&#39;re ready: React with TypeScript for websites, or Node.js with Express or Hono for servers.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Keep coming back to the playground on this site to try ideas quickly.</p>\n"},{"type":"exercise","id":"ts-6-ex1","title":"Task tracker, part 1","lang":"ts","starter":"type Priority = \"low\" | \"medium\" | \"high\";\n\ninterface Task {\n  id: number;\n  title: string;\n  priority: Priority;\n  done: boolean;\n}\n\nclass TaskTracker {\n  private tasks: Task[] = [];\n  private nextId = 1;\n\n  add(title: string, priority: Priority): Task {\n    // Your code here\n  }\n\n  complete(id: number): void {\n    // Your code here\n  }\n\n  list(filter: \"all\" | \"open\" | \"done\" = \"all\"): Task[] {\n    // Your code here\n  }\n}\n\nconst tracker = new TaskTracker();\ntracker.add(\"Learn generics\", \"high\");\ntracker.add(\"Buy milk\", \"low\");\ntracker.complete(1);\nconsole.log(tracker.list(\"open\"));\nconsole.log(tracker.list(\"done\"));","tests":"test(\"add returns a task with id 1\", () => {\n  const t = new TaskTracker();\n  expect(t.add(\"A\", \"low\")).toEqual({ id: 1, title: \"A\", priority: \"low\", done: false });\n});\ntest(\"ids count up\", () => {\n  const t = new TaskTracker();\n  t.add(\"A\", \"low\");\n  expect(t.add(\"B\", \"high\").id).toBe(2);\n});\ntest(\"empty titles throw 'Title is required'\", () => {\n  const t = new TaskTracker();\n  expect(() => t.add(\"   \", \"low\")).toThrow(\"Title is required\");\n});\ntest(\"complete marks a task done\", () => {\n  const t = new TaskTracker();\n  t.add(\"A\", \"low\");\n  t.complete(1);\n  expect(t.list()[0].done).toBe(true);\n});\ntest(\"complete with an unknown id throws 'Task not found'\", () => {\n  const t = new TaskTracker();\n  expect(() => t.complete(99)).toThrow(\"Task not found\");\n});\ntest(\"list filters open and done\", () => {\n  const t = new TaskTracker();\n  t.add(\"A\", \"low\");\n  t.add(\"B\", \"low\");\n  t.add(\"C\", \"low\");\n  t.complete(2);\n  expect(t.list(\"open\").map((x) => x.title)).toEqual([\"A\", \"C\"]);\n  expect(t.list(\"done\").map((x) => x.title)).toEqual([\"B\"]);\n  expect(t.list().length).toBe(3);\n});","solution":"type Priority = \"low\" | \"medium\" | \"high\";\n\ninterface Task {\n  id: number;\n  title: string;\n  priority: Priority;\n  done: boolean;\n}\n\nclass TaskTracker {\n  private tasks: Task[] = [];\n  private nextId = 1;\n\n  add(title: string, priority: Priority): Task {\n    if (title.trim() === \"\") {\n      throw new Error(\"Title is required\");\n    }\n    const task: Task = { id: this.nextId, title, priority, done: false };\n    this.nextId++;\n    this.tasks.push(task);\n    return task;\n  }\n\n  complete(id: number): void {\n    const task = this.tasks.find((t) => t.id === id);\n    if (!task) {\n      throw new Error(\"Task not found\");\n    }\n    task.done = true;\n  }\n\n  list(filter: \"all\" | \"open\" | \"done\" = \"all\"): Task[] {\n    if (filter === \"open\") return this.tasks.filter((t) => !t.done);\n    if (filter === \"done\") return this.tasks.filter((t) => t.done);\n    return [...this.tasks];\n  }\n}\n\nconst tracker = new TaskTracker();\ntracker.add(\"Learn generics\", \"high\");\ntracker.add(\"Buy milk\", \"low\");\ntracker.complete(1);\nconsole.log(tracker.list(\"open\"));\nconsole.log(tracker.list(\"done\"));","hints":["<p>In <code>add</code>: check <code>title.trim() === &quot;&quot;</code> first, then build a <code>Task</code> object using <code>this.nextId</code>, increase <code>nextId</code>, push the task and return it.</p>\n","<p>In <code>complete</code>: <code>const task = this.tasks.find((t) =&gt; t.id === id);</code>. TypeScript knows <code>task</code> might be <code>undefined</code>, so check it before setting <code>task.done = true</code>.</p>\n","<p>In <code>list</code>: return <code>this.tasks.filter(...)</code> for &quot;open&quot; and &quot;done&quot;, and a copy <code>[...this.tasks]</code> for &quot;all&quot;.</p>\n"],"html":"<p>Build a <code>TaskTracker</code> class. The types are given.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>add(title: string, priority: Priority): Task</code> creates a task with the next id (1, 2, 3, ...), <code>done: false</code>, stores it, and returns it. If the title is empty or only spaces, <strong>throw</strong> an <code>Error</code> with the message <code>&quot;Title is required&quot;</code>.</li>\n<li><code>complete(id: number): void</code> marks a task as done. If there&#39;s no task with that id, <strong>throw</strong> an <code>Error</code> with the message <code>&quot;Task not found&quot;</code>.</li>\n<li><code>list(filter?: &quot;all&quot; | &quot;open&quot; | &quot;done&quot;): Task[]</code> returns the tasks (default <code>&quot;all&quot;</code>), optionally only open or only done ones.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"type":"exercise","id":"ts-6-ex2","title":"Task tracker, part 2","lang":"ts","starter":"type Priority = \"low\" | \"medium\" | \"high\";\n\ninterface Task {\n  id: number;\n  title: string;\n  priority: Priority;\n  done: boolean;\n}\n\nconst PRIORITY_ORDER: Record<Priority, number> = { high: 0, medium: 1, low: 2 };\n\nclass TaskTracker {\n  private tasks: Task[] = [];\n  private nextId = 1;\n\n  add(title: string, priority: Priority): Task {\n    if (title.trim() === \"\") throw new Error(\"Title is required\");\n    const task: Task = { id: this.nextId++, title, priority, done: false };\n    this.tasks.push(task);\n    return task;\n  }\n\n  private find(id: number): Task {\n    const task = this.tasks.find((t) => t.id === id);\n    if (!task) throw new Error(\"Task not found\");\n    return task;\n  }\n\n  complete(id: number): void {\n    this.find(id).done = true;\n  }\n\n  list(filter: \"all\" | \"open\" | \"done\" = \"all\"): Task[] {\n    if (filter === \"open\") return this.tasks.filter((t) => !t.done);\n    if (filter === \"done\") return this.tasks.filter((t) => t.done);\n    return [...this.tasks];\n  }\n\n  // update(...)\n\n  // byPriority(...)\n\n  // summary(...)\n}\n\nconst tracker = new TaskTracker();\ntracker.add(\"Write report\", \"medium\");\ntracker.add(\"Fix bug\", \"high\");\ntracker.add(\"Water plants\", \"low\");\ntracker.add(\"Book dentist\", \"low\");\ntracker.update(4, { priority: \"high\" });\ntracker.complete(1);\n\nconsole.log(tracker.byPriority().map((t) => `${t.priority}: ${t.title}`));\nconsole.log(tracker.summary());","tests":"function makeTracker() {\n  const t = new TaskTracker();\n  t.add(\"Write report\", \"medium\");\n  t.add(\"Fix bug\", \"high\");\n  t.add(\"Water plants\", \"low\");\n  t.add(\"Book dentist\", \"low\");\n  return t;\n}\ntest(\"update changes fields and returns the task\", () => {\n  const t = makeTracker();\n  const u = t.update(4, { priority: \"high\", title: \"Call dentist\" });\n  expect(u).toEqual({ id: 4, title: \"Call dentist\", priority: \"high\", done: false });\n  expect(t.list()[3].priority).toBe(\"high\");\n});\ntest(\"update with an unknown id throws 'Task not found'\", () => {\n  expect(() => makeTracker().update(99, { done: true })).toThrow(\"Task not found\");\n});\ntest(\"byPriority sorts open tasks high to low\", () => {\n  const t = makeTracker();\n  expect(t.byPriority().map((x) => x.title)).toEqual([\"Fix bug\", \"Write report\", \"Water plants\", \"Book dentist\"]);\n});\ntest(\"byPriority leaves out done tasks\", () => {\n  const t = makeTracker();\n  t.complete(2);\n  expect(t.byPriority().map((x) => x.title)).toEqual([\"Write report\", \"Water plants\", \"Book dentist\"]);\n});\ntest(\"summary counts open tasks per priority\", () => {\n  const t = makeTracker();\n  t.complete(1);\n  expect(t.summary()).toEqual({ high: 1, medium: 0, low: 2 });\n});\ntest(\"summary includes priorities with 0 tasks\", () => {\n  expect(new TaskTracker().summary()).toEqual({ high: 0, medium: 0, low: 0 });\n});","solution":"type Priority = \"low\" | \"medium\" | \"high\";\n\ninterface Task {\n  id: number;\n  title: string;\n  priority: Priority;\n  done: boolean;\n}\n\nconst PRIORITY_ORDER: Record<Priority, number> = { high: 0, medium: 1, low: 2 };\n\nclass TaskTracker {\n  private tasks: Task[] = [];\n  private nextId = 1;\n\n  add(title: string, priority: Priority): Task {\n    if (title.trim() === \"\") throw new Error(\"Title is required\");\n    const task: Task = { id: this.nextId++, title, priority, done: false };\n    this.tasks.push(task);\n    return task;\n  }\n\n  private find(id: number): Task {\n    const task = this.tasks.find((t) => t.id === id);\n    if (!task) throw new Error(\"Task not found\");\n    return task;\n  }\n\n  complete(id: number): void {\n    this.find(id).done = true;\n  }\n\n  list(filter: \"all\" | \"open\" | \"done\" = \"all\"): Task[] {\n    if (filter === \"open\") return this.tasks.filter((t) => !t.done);\n    if (filter === \"done\") return this.tasks.filter((t) => t.done);\n    return [...this.tasks];\n  }\n\n  update(id: number, changes: Partial<Omit<Task, \"id\">>): Task {\n    const task = this.find(id);\n    Object.assign(task, changes);\n    return task;\n  }\n\n  byPriority(): Task[] {\n    return this.list(\"open\").sort((a, b) => PRIORITY_ORDER[a.priority] - PRIORITY_ORDER[b.priority]);\n  }\n\n  summary(): Record<Priority, number> {\n    const counts: Record<Priority, number> = { high: 0, medium: 0, low: 0 };\n    for (const task of this.list(\"open\")) {\n      counts[task.priority]++;\n    }\n    return counts;\n  }\n}\n\nconst tracker = new TaskTracker();\ntracker.add(\"Write report\", \"medium\");\ntracker.add(\"Fix bug\", \"high\");\ntracker.add(\"Water plants\", \"low\");\ntracker.add(\"Book dentist\", \"low\");\ntracker.update(4, { priority: \"high\" });\ntracker.complete(1);\n\nconsole.log(tracker.byPriority().map((t) => `${t.priority}: ${t.title}`));\nconsole.log(tracker.summary());","hints":["<p><code>update</code> can use the private <code>find</code> helper, then <code>Object.assign(task, changes)</code> copies the changes onto the task.</p>\n","<p><code>byPriority</code>: <code>this.list(&quot;open&quot;)</code> already returns a new array, so you can <code>sort</code> it directly. Compare <code>PRIORITY_ORDER[a.priority] - PRIORITY_ORDER[b.priority]</code>.</p>\n","<p><code>summary</code>: start from <code>{ high: 0, medium: 0, low: 0 }</code> and add one for each open task: <code>counts[task.priority]++</code>.</p>\n"],"html":"<p>Part 1 is finished below. Add three more methods:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>update(id: number, changes: Partial&lt;Omit&lt;Task, &quot;id&quot;&gt;&gt;): Task</code> applies the changes to the task and returns it. Throw <code>&quot;Task not found&quot;</code> for an unknown id.</li>\n<li><code>byPriority(): Task[]</code> returns <strong>open</strong> tasks sorted high, then medium, then low. Tasks with the same priority keep their order.</li>\n<li><code>summary(): Record&lt;Priority, number&gt;</code> counts <strong>open</strong> tasks per priority, like <code>{ high: 1, medium: 0, low: 2 }</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n"}]}];
